/* Go to heaven · Biology question bank (IB Biology, first assessment 2025). */
QB("bio", [
["A1.1","Explain",2,"Explain why water is a good solvent.","Water molecules are polar|polar|polarity|dipole","So they form hydrogen bonds / attractions with ions and other polar molecules, dissolving them|hydrogen bond|attract|ions|charged|dissolv"],
["A1.1","Outline",2,"Outline why water has a high specific heat capacity.","There are many hydrogen bonds between water molecules|hydrogen bond","A lot of energy is needed to break them, so its temperature changes slowly (stable habitats)|lot of energy|much energy|break|slowly|stable|resist"],
["A1.2","Distinguish",3,"Distinguish between DNA and RNA.","DNA contains deoxyribose; RNA contains ribose|deoxyribose|ribose","DNA has thymine; RNA has uracil|thymine|uracil","DNA is double-stranded; RNA is single-stranded|double|single strand|strand"],
["A1.2","State",2,"State the complementary base pairs in DNA, using full names.","Adenine pairs with thymine|adenine thymine|thymine adenine","Cytosine pairs with guanine|cytosine guanine|guanine cytosine"],
["A2.1","Outline",2,"Outline the endosymbiotic theory.","Mitochondria and chloroplasts were once free-living prokaryotes|prokaryot|bacteria|free living","That were engulfed by a larger cell (by endocytosis) and became organelles|engulf|endocytosis|taken in|absorbed|swallowed"],
["A2.1","State",2,"State two pieces of evidence for endosymbiosis.","They have their own circular DNA|circular dna|own dna|naked dna","They have 70S ribosomes|70s|ribosome","They have a double membrane|double membrane|two membranes","They divide by binary fission|binary fission|divide"],
["A2.2","Distinguish",3,"Distinguish between prokaryotic and eukaryotic cells.","Prokaryotes have no nucleus; eukaryotes have one|nucleus","Prokaryotes have no membrane-bound organelles|membrane bound|organelles","Prokaryotes have 70S ribosomes; eukaryotes have 80S|70s|80s","Prokaryotic DNA is circular and naked; eukaryotic DNA is linear with histones|circular|naked|linear|histone"],
["A2.2","State",2,"State two structures found in plant cells but not in animal cells.","Cell wall|cell wall|cellulose","Chloroplasts|chloroplast","Large permanent vacuole|vacuole"],
["A2.3","Outline",2,"Outline why viruses are not usually considered living.","They have no cell structure or metabolism of their own|no cell|not cell|acellular|no metabolism|no cytoplasm","They can only reproduce inside a host cell|host|inside a cell|only reproduce|need a cell"],
["A2.3","Distinguish",2,"Distinguish between the lytic and lysogenic cycles.","Lytic: the virus replicates and bursts (lyses) the host cell|burst|lys|destroy|kill","Lysogenic: viral DNA is inserted into the host genome and stays dormant|insert|integrat|genome|dormant|latent|prophage"],
["A3.1","Define",2,"Define species, using the biological species concept.","A group of organisms that can interbreed|interbreed|breed together|mate","To produce fertile offspring|fertile offspring|fertile"],
["A3.1","Outline",2,"Outline the binomial system of naming.","Two names: the genus then the species|genus|two names|two part","Genus starts with a capital, species is lower case, written in italics (underlined by hand)|capital|italic|underline|lower case|latin"],
["A3.2","Define",2,"Define a clade.","A group of organisms with a common ancestor|common ancestor|ancestor","Including all of its descendants|all descendants|all of its descendants|descendant"],
["A3.2","State",3,"State the three domains of life.","Bacteria|bacteria","Archaea|archaea","Eukarya / eukaryotes|eukary"],
["A4.1","Outline",3,"Outline allopatric speciation.","A population is split by a geographical barrier|geographic|barrier|separated|isolated","The groups face different selection pressures / mutations and evolve differently|different selection|different environment|evolve|mutation","Until they can no longer interbreed (reproductive isolation), forming new species|no longer interbreed|cannot interbreed|reproductive isolation|new species"],
["A4.1","State",2,"State two types of evidence for evolution.","The fossil record|fossil","DNA or protein sequence comparisons|dna|sequence|molecular|protein","Homologous structures / comparative anatomy|homologous|anatomy|pentadactyl","Selective (artificial) breeding|selective breeding|artificial selection","Observed changes, e.g. antibiotic resistance|antibiotic resistance|peppered moth|observed"],
["A4.2","Distinguish",2,"Distinguish between in situ and ex situ conservation.","In situ: protecting species in their natural habitat, e.g. nature reserves|natural habitat|nature reserve|national park|in the wild","Ex situ: conserving species outside their habitat, e.g. zoos and seed banks|outside|zoo|seed bank|captive|botanic"],
["A4.2","State",2,"State two causes of biodiversity loss.","Habitat destruction / deforestation|habitat|deforest","Pollution|pollut","Climate change|climate","Overexploitation (overhunting, overfishing)|overexploit|overhunt|overfish|poach","Invasive species|invasive|alien species"],
["B1.1","Outline",2,"Outline how a disaccharide is formed.","Two monosaccharides join|two monosaccharides|two sugars|monosaccharide","By a condensation reaction, forming a glycosidic bond and releasing water|condensation|glycosidic|water"],
["B1.1","Distinguish",2,"Distinguish between saturated and unsaturated fatty acids.","Saturated fatty acids have only single C–C bonds|single bond|no double","Unsaturated fatty acids have one or more C=C double bonds (kinks)|double bond|kink"],
["B1.2","Outline",2,"Outline the primary and secondary structure of a protein.","Primary: the sequence of amino acids|sequence|order of amino acids","Secondary: α-helices and β-pleated sheets held by hydrogen bonds|helix|helices|pleated|sheet|hydrogen bond"],
["B1.2","Define",2,"Define denaturation.","A change in a protein's 3D shape|shape|3d|structure","Caused by heat or pH, so it loses its function|heat|temperature|ph|lose function|no longer"],
["B2.1","Distinguish",2,"Distinguish between active transport and facilitated diffusion.","Active transport uses ATP and moves substances against the gradient|atp|energy|against","Facilitated diffusion uses no ATP and moves down the gradient through channel or carrier proteins|no energy|passive|down|channel|carrier"],
["B2.1","Define",2,"Define osmosis.","The net movement of water|water","Across a partially permeable membrane, from lower to higher solute concentration|partially permeable|semi permeable|membrane|higher solute|lower solute|water potential"],
["B2.2","State",2,"State the function of mitochondria and of ribosomes.","Mitochondria: site of aerobic respiration, producing ATP|aerobic respiration|cell respiration|cellular respiration|site of respiration|atp","Ribosomes: site of protein synthesis|protein synthesis|make protein|make proteins|translation|synthesise proteins|synthesize proteins"],
["B2.2","Explain",2,"Explain one advantage of compartmentalization in eukaryotic cells.","Harmful or conflicting reactions and enzymes are kept apart, e.g. digestive enzymes in lysosomes|separate|lysosome|digestive enzymes|harmful|conflicting|apart","Each compartment can keep ideal conditions or concentrations for its reactions|ideal|optimal|concentration|efficient|specific conditions"],
["B2.3","Define",2,"Define stem cell.","An unspecialized (undifferentiated) cell|unspecialis|unspecializ|undifferentiat","That can divide and differentiate into specialized cell types|differentiat|specialised|specialized|divide|self renew"],
["B2.3","Explain",2,"Explain why cell size is limited by the surface area to volume ratio.","As a cell grows, its volume increases faster than its surface area|volume increases faster|ratio decreases|ratio falls|smaller ratio","So exchange across the membrane can no longer meet the cell's needs|exchange|diffusion|not enough|cannot supply|remove waste"],
["B3.1","State",2,"State two adaptations of alveoli for gas exchange.","Large surface area|surface area","Thin walls, one cell thick|thin|one cell","Moist lining|moist","Dense capillary network / good blood supply|capillar|blood supply","Surfactant|surfactant"],
["B3.1","Outline",3,"Outline the process of inhalation.","The diaphragm contracts and flattens|diaphragm","The external intercostal muscles contract, raising the ribs|intercostal|ribs","The volume of the thorax increases and pressure falls, so air flows in|volume increase|pressure decrease|pressure fall|lower pressure|air flows in|air in"],
["B3.2","Distinguish",3,"Distinguish between arteries and veins.","Arteries have thick, muscular, elastic walls; veins have thin walls|thick|thin|muscular|elastic","Arteries have a narrow lumen; veins have a wide lumen|lumen","Veins have valves; arteries do not|valve","Arteries carry blood at high pressure away from the heart|high pressure|away from the heart"],
["B3.2","Outline",2,"Outline how water moves up the xylem.","Transpiration from the leaves creates tension that pulls water up|transpiration|tension|pull|suction","Cohesion between water molecules and adhesion to the xylem walls|cohesion|adhesion|hydrogen bond"],
["B3.3","Outline",3,"Outline the sliding filament model of muscle contraction.","Myosin heads bind to actin, forming cross-bridges|myosin|actin|cross bridge","ATP is used to pull the actin filaments towards the centre (power stroke)|atp|power stroke|pull","The sarcomere shortens|sarcomere|shorten"],
["B3.3","State",2,"State the role of calcium ions in muscle contraction.","They are released from the sarcoplasmic reticulum|sarcoplasmic reticulum","They bind to troponin, moving tropomyosin to expose the binding sites on actin|troponin|tropomyosin|binding site|expose"],
["B4.1","State",2,"State two adaptations of desert plants (xerophytes).","Thick waxy cuticle|wax|cuticle","Few or sunken stomata|stomata","Small leaves or spines|spine|small leaves|needle","Deep or wide roots|root","Water storage tissue|store water|succulent"],
["B4.1","Define",2,"Define habitat.","The place where an organism lives|place|where|lives|location","Including its environmental conditions|conditions|environment|abiotic"],
["B4.2","Outline",2,"Outline the competitive exclusion principle.","Two species cannot occupy the same niche|same niche|identical niche","In the same place for long: one outcompetes the other, which dies out or moves|outcompete|die out|extinct|excluded|one wins"],
["B4.2","Distinguish",2,"Distinguish between fundamental and realized niches.","Fundamental niche: the full range of conditions a species could occupy|could|potential|full range|without competition","Realized niche: the part it actually occupies because of competition|actually|competition|smaller|limited"],
["C1.1","Explain",3,"Explain the effect of high temperature on enzyme activity.","Activity rises up to the optimum as molecules move faster and collide more|optimum|collision|collide|kinetic energy|faster","Above the optimum, bonds holding the enzyme's shape break|bonds break|hydrogen bonds|vibrat","The active site changes shape, the enzyme is denatured and activity falls|denature|active site|shape"],
["C1.1","Distinguish",2,"Distinguish between competitive and non-competitive inhibition.","Competitive inhibitors bind to the active site|active site","Non-competitive inhibitors bind elsewhere (an allosteric site), changing the active site's shape|allosteric|elsewhere|another site|different site|change shape"],
["C1.2","Distinguish",3,"Distinguish between aerobic and anaerobic respiration in humans.","Aerobic respiration needs oxygen; anaerobic does not|oxygen","Aerobic produces carbon dioxide and water; anaerobic produces lactate|carbon dioxide|water|lactate|lactic acid","Aerobic yields far more ATP|more atp|36|38|2 atp"],
["C1.2","State",2,"State where glycolysis and the Krebs cycle take place.","Glycolysis: the cytoplasm|cytoplasm","Krebs cycle: the mitochondrial matrix|matrix|mitochondri"],
["C1.3","State",2,"State the word equation for photosynthesis.","Carbon dioxide + water (with light energy)|carbon dioxide water|co2 water|water carbon dioxide","→ glucose + oxygen|glucose oxygen|sugar oxygen|oxygen glucose"],
["C1.3","State",2,"State two limiting factors of photosynthesis.","Light intensity|light","Carbon dioxide concentration|carbon dioxide|co2","Temperature|temperature"],
["C2.1","Outline",2,"Outline how a hormone reaches and affects its target cells.","It is secreted by a gland into the blood|blood|gland|secret","It binds to specific receptors on or in its target cells|receptor|target|specific"],
["C2.1","Distinguish",2,"Distinguish between how steroid and peptide hormones act.","Steroid hormones pass through the membrane and bind to receptors inside the cell|through the membrane|inside|intracellular|nucleus|lipid soluble","Peptide hormones bind to receptors on the cell surface and act through second messengers|surface|membrane receptor|second messenger|camp"],
["C2.2","Outline",3,"Outline how a nerve impulse passes across a synapse.","The impulse causes neurotransmitter to be released from vesicles|neurotransmitter|vesicle|release","The neurotransmitter diffuses across the synaptic cleft|diffuse|cleft|gap","It binds to receptors on the postsynaptic membrane, starting a new impulse|receptor|postsynaptic|bind"],
["C2.2","Define",2,"Define resting potential.","The potential difference across a neuron's membrane when it is not transmitting|potential difference|membrane|at rest|not transmitting","About −70 mV, maintained by the sodium–potassium pump|70|sodium potassium|pump"],
["C3.1","Outline",2,"Outline the roles of the nervous and endocrine systems in coordinating the body.","Nervous system: fast, short-lived electrical impulses along neurons|nerve|neuron|electrical|fast|impulse","Endocrine system: slower, longer-lasting hormones carried in the blood|hormone|blood|slow|longer"],
["C3.1","Define",1,"Define reflex.","A rapid, involuntary response to a stimulus|involuntary|automatic|without thinking|unconscious"],
["C3.2","Distinguish",2,"Distinguish between innate and adaptive immunity.","Innate immunity is non-specific and the same for all pathogens (e.g. skin, phagocytes)|non specific|nonspecific|same|phagocyte|skin|general","Adaptive immunity is specific to one pathogen, uses lymphocytes and antibodies, and has memory|specific|lymphocyte|antibod|memory"],
["C3.2","Explain",2,"Explain how vaccination gives immunity.","The vaccine contains antigens from a weakened or dead pathogen|antigen|weakened|dead|inactiv","It triggers memory cells, so a later infection gets a fast, strong response|memory cell|memory|faster response|secondary response"],
["C4.1","State",2,"State two density-dependent factors that limit population growth.","Competition for food or space|competition|food","Predation|predat","Disease and parasites|disease|parasite"],
["C4.1","Outline",2,"Outline how capture–mark–release–recapture estimates the size of an animal population.","A first sample is caught, marked harmlessly and released to mix back in|harmless|harmlessly|paint|tag|tagged|mix|first sample","A second sample is caught later, and population = (first × second) ÷ number of marked animals recaptured|second sample|lincoln|multiply|multiplied|divide|divided|×|÷"],
["C4.2","Explain",2,"Explain why food chains rarely have more than five trophic levels.","Energy is lost at each level, mostly as heat from respiration and in waste|energy lost|heat|respiration|waste|10","So too little energy is left to support higher levels|not enough|too little|insufficient"],
["C4.2","State",2,"State two processes that release carbon dioxide into the atmosphere.","Respiration|respiration","Combustion / burning fossil fuels|combust|burn","Decomposition|decompos","Volcanic eruptions|volcan"],
["D1.1","Define",2,"Define semi-conservative replication.","Each new DNA molecule keeps one original strand|one original|original strand|parent strand|old strand","And one newly made strand|new strand|newly"],
["D1.1","State",2,"State the roles of helicase and DNA polymerase.","Helicase unwinds and separates the strands by breaking hydrogen bonds|unwind|separate|hydrogen bond|unzip","DNA polymerase adds complementary nucleotides to build the new strand|nucleotide|new strand|complementary|join"],
["D1.2","Outline",2,"Outline transcription.","A DNA strand is used as a template to make mRNA|mrna|template","By RNA polymerase, in the nucleus|rna polymerase|nucleus"],
["D1.2","Outline",2,"Outline translation.","A ribosome reads the mRNA in codons|ribosome|codon","tRNA brings specific amino acids, joined by peptide bonds into a polypeptide|trna|anticodon|amino acid|peptide bond|polypeptide"],
["D1.3","Define",2,"Define gene mutation.","A change in the base sequence|base sequence|change in dna|dna sequence|nucleotide sequence","Of a gene, which may produce a new allele|base sequence of a gene|sequence of a gene|dna of a gene|new allele|allele"],
["D1.3","Outline",2,"Outline how CRISPR–Cas9 is used in gene editing.","A guide RNA matches the target DNA sequence|guide rna|grna|target","Cas9 cuts the DNA there so genes can be removed, changed or inserted|cas9|cut|insert|remove|edit"],
["D2.1","Distinguish",3,"Distinguish between mitosis and meiosis.","Mitosis produces two cells; meiosis produces four|two|four","Mitosis gives identical diploid cells; meiosis gives haploid cells|diploid|haploid|identical","Meiosis creates variation by crossing over and independent assortment|variation|crossing over|independent assortment|different"],
["D2.1","State",2,"State two stages of mitosis.","Prophase|prophase","Metaphase|metaphase","Anaphase|anaphase","Telophase|telophase"],
["D2.2","Outline",2,"Outline how DNA methylation affects gene expression.","Methyl groups are added to DNA (usually cytosine)|methyl","This usually switches genes off / reduces transcription|switch off|silenc|reduce|less transcription|inhibit"],
["D2.2","Define",2,"Define epigenetics.","Heritable changes in gene expression|gene expression|heritable","Without any change to the DNA base sequence|without|no change|base sequence"],
["D2.3","Outline",2,"Outline what happens to a plant cell placed in a hypotonic solution.","Water enters the cell by osmosis|water enters|water moves in|osmosis","The cell becomes turgid; the cell wall stops it bursting|turgid|swell|wall"],
["D2.3","Outline",2,"Outline what happens to an animal cell placed in a hypertonic solution.","Water leaves the cell by osmosis|water leaves|water moves out|osmosis","The cell shrinks (becomes crenated)|shrink|crenat|shrivel"],
["D3.1","Distinguish",2,"Distinguish between sexual and asexual reproduction.","Sexual: gametes from two parents fuse, so offspring are genetically varied|two parents|gametes|fertilis|fertiliz|varied|variation","Asexual: one parent, so offspring are genetically identical clones|one parent|single parent|identical|clone"],
["D3.1","State",2,"State two hormones involved in the menstrual cycle.","FSH|fsh|follicle stimulating","LH|lh|luteinising|luteinizing","Oestrogen|oestrogen|estrogen","Progesterone|progesterone"],
["D3.2","Distinguish",2,"Distinguish between genotype and phenotype.","Genotype: the alleles an organism has|allele|genetic makeup|genes","Phenotype: its observable characteristics|observable|characteristics|traits|appearance|physical"],
["D3.2","Define",2,"Define codominance.","Both alleles are expressed|both alleles|both expressed","In the phenotype of a heterozygote, e.g. blood group AB|heterozygo|ab blood|phenotype"],
["D3.3","Outline",3,"Outline how blood glucose is lowered after a meal.","Beta cells in the pancreas detect high glucose and secrete insulin|insulin|pancreas|beta","Insulin makes liver and muscle cells take up glucose|take up|absorb|uptake|liver|muscle","Glucose is converted to glycogen for storage|glycogen"],
["D3.3","Define",2,"Define homeostasis.","Keeping the internal environment constant|constant|stable|internal environment","Within narrow limits despite external changes, using negative feedback|limits|negative feedback|despite"],
["D4.1","Outline",3,"Outline natural selection.","There is variation within a population (from mutation)|variation|vary","Individuals with useful traits survive and reproduce more|survive|reproduce|advantage|better adapted|fittest","They pass on their alleles, so allele frequencies change over generations|pass on|allele|frequency|generations|offspring"],
["D4.1","Explain",2,"Explain how antibiotic resistance spreads in bacteria.","A random mutation makes some bacteria resistant|mutation|random","Antibiotics kill the others, so resistant bacteria survive and multiply|survive|reproduce|multiply|selection|kill"],
["D4.2","Define",2,"Define keystone species.","A species with a disproportionately large effect on its ecosystem|large effect|disproportionate|important","Whose removal would change the whole community|removal|collapse|change|ecosystem"],
["D4.2","Outline",2,"Outline primary succession.","It starts on bare ground with no soil, e.g. new volcanic rock|bare|no soil|rock|volcan","Pioneer species colonise, soil forms and communities change until a climax community|pioneer|lichen|moss|soil|climax"],
["D4.3","Explain",2,"Explain how the enhanced greenhouse effect causes global warming.","Human activity increases greenhouse gases such as CO₂ and methane|human|greenhouse gas|carbon dioxide|co2|methane","These trap more of the infrared (long-wave) radiation emitted by the Earth|trap|infrared|longwave|long wave|radiation|heat"],
["D4.3","State",2,"State two effects of climate change on ecosystems.","Species ranges shift towards the poles or uphill|range|shift|poleward|higher altitude","Coral bleaching|coral","Loss of sea-ice habitat for polar species|ice|polar","Mismatched timing of events (phenology)|timing|phenolog","Extinctions|extinct"],
]);

/* ---------- more questions, each with a model answer and an examiner's tip ---------- */
QB("bio", [
["A1.1","Explain",2,"Explain how cohesion between water molecules helps water move up the xylem.","Hydrogen bonds hold water molecules together|hydrogen bond|hydrogen bonds","So water is pulled up as a continuous column under tension as water evaporates from leaves|continuous column|column|pulled|tension|transpiration",{model:"Hydrogen bonds hold water molecules together, so when water evaporates from the leaves, the continuous column of water in the xylem is pulled up under tension.",tip:"Name the bond (hydrogen) and the effect (a continuous column pulled up)."}],
["A1.1","Outline",2,"Outline why ice floats on liquid water.","Ice is less dense than liquid water|less dense|lower density|density","Hydrogen bonds hold the molecules further apart in an open lattice|lattice|further apart|spaced out|hydrogen bond",{model:"When water freezes, hydrogen bonds hold the molecules in an open lattice, further apart than in liquid water, so ice is less dense and floats.",tip:"Density is the key word; the lattice explains it."}],
["A1.2","Outline",3,"Outline the structure of a DNA nucleotide.","A phosphate group|phosphate","A deoxyribose sugar|deoxyribose|pentose","A nitrogenous base: adenine, thymine, cytosine or guanine|nitrogenous base|base|adenine|thymine|guanine|cytosine",{model:"A DNA nucleotide is made of a phosphate group, a deoxyribose sugar and a nitrogenous base (adenine, thymine, cytosine or guanine).",tip:"Three marks, three parts: phosphate, deoxyribose, base."}],
["A1.2","State",2,"State how the two strands of a DNA molecule are held together.","By hydrogen bonds|hydrogen bond|hydrogen bonds","Between complementary base pairs, A with T and C with G|complementary|base pairs|base pairing|adenine",{model:"The two strands are held together by hydrogen bonds between complementary base pairs: adenine with thymine and cytosine with guanine.",tip:"Mention both the bond type and the base pairing."}],
["A2.1","Outline",2,"Outline the Miller–Urey experiment.","It simulated the early Earth's atmosphere (methane, ammonia, hydrogen, water) with electric sparks as lightning|methane|ammonia|sparks|lightning|electric","Amino acids and other organic molecules formed from inorganic ones|amino acids|organic",{model:"Miller and Urey passed electric sparks, simulating lightning, through a mixture of methane, ammonia, hydrogen and water vapour like the early atmosphere. Amino acids and other organic molecules formed.",tip:"Describe the set-up and the result."}],
["A2.1","Outline",2,"Outline the RNA world hypothesis.","RNA was the first genetic material, before DNA|first|before dna|earliest|genetic material","Because RNA can both store information and catalyse reactions (ribozymes)|catalyse|catalyze|ribozyme|ribozymes|enzyme",{model:"The hypothesis is that RNA was the first genetic material, before DNA and proteins, because RNA can both store information and act as a catalyst (ribozymes).",tip:"The double role of RNA (information and catalysis) is the core argument."}],
["A2.2","Outline",2,"Outline the functions of the cell wall in plant cells.","It provides support and strength, keeping the cell's shape|support|strength|shape|rigid","It stops the cell bursting by resisting turgor pressure|bursting|burst|turgor|pressure",{model:"The cellulose cell wall supports the cell and keeps its shape, and it resists turgor pressure so the cell does not burst when water enters.",tip:"Give two functions, one sentence each."}],
["A2.2","Outline",2,"Outline why electron microscopes show more detail than light microscopes.","Electrons have a much shorter wavelength than light|wavelength","So they give a higher resolution|resolution|resolving",{model:"Electrons have a much shorter wavelength than light, so electron microscopes have a much higher resolution and can separate smaller structures.",tip:"Detail means resolution, not magnification."}],
["A2.3","State",2,"State two structural features common to all viruses.","Genetic material, either DNA or RNA|genetic material|dna|rna|nucleic acid","A protein coat (capsid)|capsid|protein coat|coat",{model:"Every virus has genetic material (DNA or RNA) inside a protein coat called a capsid.",tip:"Avoid features only some viruses have, such as an envelope."}],
["A2.3","Explain",2,"Explain why antibiotics are not effective against viruses.","Antibiotics target bacterial structures or processes, such as cell walls or bacterial ribosomes|cell wall|cell walls|bacterial|ribosomes","Viruses lack these and use the host cell's machinery|host cell|host|lack|no metabolism",{model:"Antibiotics work by attacking structures and processes in bacteria, such as cell walls or 70S ribosomes. Viruses have none of these: they reproduce using the host cell's machinery.",tip:"Explain what antibiotics target, then why viruses don't have it."}],
["A3.1","Outline",2,"Outline one difficulty with applying the biological species concept.","It cannot be used for organisms that reproduce asexually, such as bacteria|asexual|asexually|bacteria","Or for fossils, or populations that never meet or form hybrids|fossils|fossil|extinct|never meet|hybrids",{model:"The concept depends on interbreeding, so it cannot be used for organisms that reproduce asexually, such as bacteria, or for extinct species known only as fossils.",tip:"Link the difficulty to the definition: it relies on interbreeding."}],
["A3.1","Outline",2,"Outline what a dichotomous key is used for.","To identify an organism|identify|identification","By working through a series of paired choices about its features|paired|two choices|pairs|series|features|characteristics",{model:"A dichotomous key is used to identify an organism by working through a series of paired choices about its visible features.",tip:"Say what it does (identifies) and how (paired choices)."}],
["A3.2","Outline",2,"Outline how molecular data are used to construct a cladogram.","Base or amino acid sequences of species are compared|compared|compare|base sequence|amino acid sequence","The fewer the differences, the more recent the common ancestor, so they are placed closer together|fewer differences|common ancestor|more recent|closely related",{model:"The base sequences of a gene (or the amino acid sequences of a protein) are compared between species. Species with fewer differences share a more recent common ancestor, so they branch closer together.",tip:"Connect the number of differences to how recently the species diverged."}],
["A3.2","Distinguish",2,"Distinguish between homologous and analogous structures.","Homologous structures share a common ancestry and basic structure, even if their functions differ|common ancestor|ancestry|same basic structure|pentadactyl","Analogous structures have the same function but evolved separately (convergent evolution)|same function|convergent|evolved separately|independently",{model:"Homologous structures, such as the pentadactyl limb, share a common ancestry and basic structure but can have different functions. Analogous structures, such as bird and insect wings, do the same job but evolved separately.",tip:"An example of each makes the difference clear."}],
["A4.1","Outline",2,"Outline how selective breeding provides evidence for evolution.","Humans choose individuals with desired traits to breed (artificial selection)|humans|artificial|desired|chosen|choose","Large changes appear in a species over relatively few generations, showing species can change|generations|large changes|changed|short time|differ",{model:"In artificial selection, humans choose individuals with desired traits to breed. Breeds such as dogs changed greatly in a few hundred generations, showing that species can change, as evolution proposes.",tip:"Make the link: if humans can change a species quickly, natural selection can over longer times."}],
["A4.1","Define",2,"Define the term speciation.","The formation of a new species|new species|formation","When populations become reproductively isolated and diverge|isolated|isolation|diverge|interbreed",{model:"Speciation is the formation of a new species, when populations become reproductively isolated and diverge until they can no longer interbreed.",tip:"Include reproductive isolation: it is what makes a new species."}],
["A4.2","Outline",2,"Outline how rewilding can help restore biodiversity.","Missing species, such as top predators, are reintroduced or land is left to return to a natural state|reintroduce|reintroduced|reintroduction|predators|natural state","This restores food webs and natural processes so more species can live there|food web|food webs|natural processes|more species|ecosystem",{model:"Rewilding reintroduces missing species, such as wolves or beavers, and lets land return to a natural state. This restores food webs and natural processes, so more species can survive there.",tip:"A named example such as wolves in Yellowstone earns credibility."}],
["A4.2","Explain",2,"Explain why habitat loss reduces biodiversity.","Species lose their food, shelter or breeding sites|food|shelter|breeding|resources","So their populations shrink, become fragmented or go extinct|extinct|extinction|shrink|fragmented|decline",{model:"When a habitat is destroyed, the species living there lose their food, shelter and breeding sites, so their populations decline, become fragmented or go extinct.",tip:"Cause, then consequence for populations."}],
["B1.1","Distinguish",2,"Distinguish between amylose and cellulose.","Amylose is made of alpha-glucose; cellulose of beta-glucose|alpha|beta","Amylose is coiled (helical) for storage; cellulose forms straight chains bundled into fibres for structure|coiled|helix|helical|straight|fibres|fibers",{model:"Amylose is a polymer of alpha-glucose that coils into a compact helix for energy storage. Cellulose is a polymer of beta-glucose that forms straight chains bundled into strong fibres for structure.",tip:"Monomer, shape and function: compare all three."}],
["B1.1","Outline",2,"Outline why triglycerides are used for long-term energy storage.","They release about twice as much energy per gram as carbohydrates|twice|per gram|energy dense|more energy","They are insoluble, so they don't affect osmosis, and are light for the energy stored|insoluble|osmosis|light|lighter|compact",{model:"Triglycerides release about twice as much energy per gram as carbohydrates, and they are insoluble, so large stores do not affect osmosis and add little mass.",tip:"Energy per gram is the mark examiners always look for."}],
["B1.2","Outline",2,"Outline how a peptide bond forms.","By a condensation reaction|condensation","Between the amine group of one amino acid and the carboxyl group of another, releasing water|carboxyl|amine|amino group|water|cooh",{model:"A peptide bond forms in a condensation reaction between the carboxyl group of one amino acid and the amine group of another, releasing a molecule of water.",tip:"Name the reaction type and the two groups involved."}],
["B1.2","Explain",2,"Explain how the shape of a protein depends on its amino acid sequence.","The sequence determines where bonds form between R groups (hydrogen, ionic, disulfide)|bonds|interactions|hydrogen|disulfide|ionic|r groups","Which fold the chain into a specific 3D (tertiary) shape|fold|folding|tertiary|3d|three dimensional",{model:"The amino acid sequence decides which R groups are next to each other, and so where hydrogen bonds, ionic bonds and disulfide bridges form. These fold the chain into its specific 3D tertiary shape.",tip:"Go from sequence → bonds → folding → shape."}],
["B2.1","Outline",2,"Outline the role of cholesterol in animal cell membranes.","It regulates the fluidity of the membrane|fluidity|fluid","It keeps the membrane stable and reduces its permeability to small ions|stable|stability|permeability|rigid",{model:"Cholesterol sits between the phospholipids and regulates membrane fluidity, keeping it stable across temperatures and reducing its permeability to small ions.",tip:"Fluidity is the key word."}],
["B2.1","Explain",2,"Explain why phospholipids form a bilayer in water.","Phospholipids have hydrophilic heads and hydrophobic tails|hydrophilic|hydrophobic","The heads face the water on each side, while the tails face inwards, away from water|face|inwards|inward|away from water|tails",{model:"Phospholipids have hydrophilic phosphate heads and hydrophobic fatty acid tails. In water they form a bilayer with the heads facing the water on both sides and the tails pointing inwards, away from it.",tip:"Use the words hydrophilic and hydrophobic."}],
["B2.2","State",2,"State the functions of the rough endoplasmic reticulum and the Golgi apparatus.","Rough ER: proteins are made on its ribosomes, often for secretion|synthesis|synthesise|synthesize|make proteins|ribosomes","Golgi apparatus: modifies, sorts and packages proteins into vesicles|modifies|packages|packaging|vesicles|sorts",{model:"The rough ER makes proteins on its ribosomes, often for secretion. The Golgi apparatus modifies, sorts and packages these proteins into vesicles.",tip:"One function for each organelle."}],
["B2.2","Outline",2,"Outline why the nucleus is separated from the cytoplasm by a nuclear envelope.","It keeps transcription separate from translation|transcription|translation","So mRNA can be processed (modified) before it is translated, and DNA is protected|processed|processing|modified|splicing|protect|protects",{model:"The nuclear envelope keeps transcription in the nucleus separate from translation in the cytoplasm, so mRNA can be processed before it is translated, and the DNA is protected.",tip:"Compartmentalisation: say which two processes it separates."}],
["B2.3","Outline",2,"Outline how stem cells become specialized.","Some genes are switched on (expressed) and others switched off|genes|expressed|expression|switched","This differentiation gives the cell a specific structure and function|differentiation|differentiate|specific function",{model:"During differentiation, certain genes are expressed while others are switched off, so the cell develops the specific structure and function of one cell type.",tip:"Gene expression is the mechanism behind differentiation."}],
["B2.3","Outline",2,"Outline two adaptations of red blood cells for carrying oxygen.","No nucleus, leaving more room for haemoglobin|nucleus|room|space","Biconcave shape gives a large surface area for diffusion|biconcave|surface area","Full of haemoglobin, which binds oxygen|haemoglobin|hemoglobin","Small and flexible, to pass through capillaries|flexible|capillaries|small",{model:"Red blood cells have no nucleus, leaving more room for haemoglobin, and a biconcave shape that gives a large surface area for oxygen to diffuse in.",tip:"Pair each feature with how it helps."}],
["B3.1","Outline",2,"Outline the role of surfactant in the lungs.","It reduces surface tension in the alveoli|surface tension","Stopping the alveoli collapsing or sticking together|collapse|collapsing|sticking",{model:"Surfactant reduces the surface tension of the moisture lining the alveoli, which stops them collapsing and sticking together when we breathe out.",tip:"Two marks: what it reduces, and what that prevents."}],
["B3.1","Outline",2,"Outline two adaptations of leaves for gas exchange.","Stomata let gases in and out|stomata|stoma","Air spaces in the spongy mesophyll|air spaces|spongy","A thin, flat shape gives a large surface area and a short diffusion distance|thin|flat|surface area|short distance",{model:"Stomata let carbon dioxide in and oxygen out, and air spaces in the spongy mesophyll let gases diffuse to every cell.",tip:"Pick two clearly different features."}],
["B3.2","Outline",2,"Outline how the structure of capillaries suits their function.","Their walls are one cell thick, giving a short diffusion distance|one cell|thin|short diffusion","They have pores (fenestrations) that let tissue fluid form|pores|fenestrations|gaps|leaky","They are narrow and highly branched, giving a large surface area|narrow|surface area|branched|network",{model:"Capillary walls are one cell thick, so substances diffuse only a short distance, and the capillaries form a highly branched network with a large surface area for exchange.",tip:"Structure → function, for each feature."}],
["B3.2","Outline",2,"Outline how phloem transports sugars.","Sucrose is actively loaded into the phloem at the source|loaded|loading|source|active","Water follows by osmosis, raising the pressure, which pushes sap towards the sink (mass flow)|pressure|osmosis|mass flow|sink",{model:"Sucrose is actively loaded into the phloem at a source such as a leaf. Water follows by osmosis, raising the pressure, which pushes the sap by mass flow to a sink such as a root.",tip:"Use the terms source, sink and mass flow."}],
["B3.3","Outline",2,"Outline the role of ATP in muscle contraction.","ATP binding makes the myosin head detach from actin|detach|release|unbind|breaks","Hydrolysis of ATP re-cocks the myosin head so it can bind again and pull|hydrolysis|cocks|cocked|energy|power stroke|reset",{model:"ATP binds to the myosin head, making it detach from actin. Hydrolysis of the ATP then re-cocks the head so it can bind further along the actin and pull again.",tip:"Two steps: detaching, then re-cocking."}],
["B3.3","Explain",2,"Explain why skeletal muscles work in antagonistic pairs.","Muscles can only pull by contracting; they cannot push|only pull|cannot push|pull","So one muscle contracts while the other relaxes, moving the bone back, e.g. biceps and triceps|relaxes|biceps|triceps|other muscle|opposite",{model:"Muscles can only pull, so a joint needs one muscle to bend it and another to straighten it: when the biceps contracts, the triceps relaxes, and the reverse.",tip:"An example pair such as biceps and triceps secures the mark."}],
["B4.1","Outline",2,"Outline two adaptations of hydrophytes (aquatic plants).","Air spaces (aerenchyma) for buoyancy and oxygen|air spaces|aerenchyma|buoyancy|float","Stomata on the upper surface of floating leaves|upper surface|stomata","A thin cuticle or none|cuticle","Large, flat floating leaves to catch light|large leaves|floating leaves|flat",{model:"Hydrophytes have air spaces (aerenchyma) that keep them afloat and store oxygen, and their floating leaves have stomata on the upper surface.",tip:"Contrast with xerophytes if it helps you remember."}],
["B4.1","Define",2,"Define the term abiotic factor and give one example.","A non-living feature of the environment|non living|nonliving|not living|physical factor|chemical factor","For example temperature, light, water, pH or salinity|temperature|light|water|ph|salinity|rainfall",{model:"An abiotic factor is a non-living feature of the environment, such as temperature, light intensity or soil pH.",tip:"Definition plus example: the question asks for both."}],
["B4.2","Distinguish",2,"Distinguish between autotrophs and heterotrophs.","Autotrophs make their own organic molecules from inorganic substances, e.g. by photosynthesis|own food|own organic|photosynthesis|inorganic|make their own","Heterotrophs obtain organic molecules from other organisms|other organisms|eat|consume|obtain",{model:"Autotrophs make their own organic molecules from inorganic substances, for example by photosynthesis. Heterotrophs obtain organic molecules by consuming other organisms.",tip:"Source of organic molecules is the difference."}],
["B4.2","Outline",2,"Outline what is meant by an ecological niche.","The role a species plays in its ecosystem|role","Including its habitat, interactions with other species and the resources it uses|habitat|interactions|resources|food|feeding",{model:"A niche is the role a species plays in its ecosystem: where it lives, what it feeds on, how it interacts with other species and which resources it uses.",tip:"A niche is more than a habitat: include its role and interactions."}],
["C1.1","Outline",2,"Outline the induced-fit model of enzyme action.","The active site changes shape slightly as the substrate binds|changes shape|alters|adjusts|moulds","To fit the substrate more closely, putting strain on its bonds|strain|stress|weaken|tightly|closely",{model:"When the substrate binds, the active site changes shape slightly to fit it more closely, which strains the substrate's bonds and lowers the activation energy.",tip:"Contrast it with the rigid lock-and-key model if asked."}],
["C1.1","Explain",2,"Explain the effect of increasing substrate concentration on the rate of an enzyme-catalysed reaction.","The rate rises because enzyme–substrate collisions become more frequent|collisions|more frequent|faster","Until all active sites are occupied (saturation), when the rate levels off|saturated|saturation|all active sites|plateau|levels off",{model:"At first the rate rises because more frequent collisions form more enzyme–substrate complexes. Once all active sites are occupied, the enzyme is saturated and the rate levels off.",tip:"Explain both parts of the curve: rising and levelling off."}],
["C1.2","Outline",2,"Outline the role of oxygen in aerobic respiration.","It is the final electron acceptor in the electron transport chain|final electron acceptor|electron acceptor|accepts electrons","It combines with electrons and protons to form water, so the chain and ATP production can continue|water|continue|keeps going|h2o",{model:"Oxygen is the final electron acceptor at the end of the electron transport chain. It combines with electrons and protons to form water, which keeps the chain, and ATP production, going.",tip:"'Final electron acceptor' is the phrase markschemes use."}],
["C1.2","State",2,"State the products of anaerobic respiration in yeast.","Ethanol|ethanol|alcohol","Carbon dioxide|carbon dioxide|co2",{model:"Ethanol and carbon dioxide (with a small amount of ATP).",tip:"Don't confuse yeast (ethanol and CO2) with humans (lactate)."}],
["C1.3","Outline",2,"Outline the role of chlorophyll in photosynthesis.","It absorbs light energy, mainly red and blue light|absorbs|absorb|light","Excited electrons are passed on, converting light energy into chemical energy|electrons|excited|chemical energy|converts",{model:"Chlorophyll absorbs light, mainly red and blue wavelengths. The energy excites electrons, which are passed along so the light energy is converted into chemical energy.",tip:"Say what it absorbs and what happens to the energy."}],
["C1.3","Distinguish",2,"Distinguish between the light-dependent and light-independent reactions of photosynthesis.","The light-dependent reactions happen in the thylakoids and produce ATP and NADPH, releasing oxygen|thylakoid|thylakoids|nadph|oxygen","The light-independent reactions (Calvin cycle) happen in the stroma and fix carbon dioxide into sugars|stroma|calvin|fixes|fixed|carbon dioxide|sugars",{model:"The light-dependent reactions take place in the thylakoids, using light to make ATP and NADPH and release oxygen. The Calvin cycle takes place in the stroma and uses them to fix carbon dioxide into sugars.",tip:"Location and products for each stage."}],
["C2.1","Define",2,"Define the term ligand and give an example.","A signalling molecule that binds specifically to a receptor|binds|receptor|signalling molecule","For example a hormone, neurotransmitter or cytokine|hormone|neurotransmitter|cytokine|insulin|adrenaline",{model:"A ligand is a signalling molecule that binds specifically to a receptor, for example the hormone insulin or the neurotransmitter acetylcholine.",tip:"Definition plus a named example."}],
["C2.1","Outline",2,"Outline the role of a second messenger such as cyclic AMP.","A hormone binding to a membrane receptor triggers production of cAMP inside the cell|inside|triggers|production|membrane receptor","cAMP activates enzymes in a cascade that amplifies the response|activates|cascade|amplify|amplifies|amplification|enzymes",{model:"When a hormone such as adrenaline binds to a receptor on the membrane, cAMP is produced inside the cell. cAMP activates a cascade of enzymes that amplifies the response.",tip:"The hormone stays outside; the second messenger carries the signal inside."}],
["C2.2","Outline",2,"Outline how myelination increases the speed of nerve impulses.","Myelin insulates the axon|insulates|insulation|insulating","So the impulse jumps between the nodes of Ranvier (saltatory conduction)|nodes|ranvier|saltatory|jumps",{model:"Myelin insulates the axon, so depolarisation happens only at the gaps, the nodes of Ranvier, and the impulse jumps from node to node (saltatory conduction).",tip:"Use the term saltatory conduction."}],
["C2.2","Outline",2,"Outline depolarization during an action potential.","Voltage-gated sodium channels open|sodium channels|sodium|na","Sodium ions diffuse in, so the inside of the axon becomes positive|diffuse in|rush in|positive|inside",{model:"Voltage-gated sodium channels open, sodium ions diffuse into the axon, and the inside becomes positive compared with the outside.",tip:"Name the ion and its direction."}],
["C3.1","Outline",2,"Outline the role of the hypothalamus in linking the nervous and endocrine systems.","It receives nerve signals and monitors conditions in the body|receives|monitors|detects|senses","It controls the pituitary gland, which releases hormones|pituitary",{model:"The hypothalamus monitors the body through nerve signals and blood, and it controls the pituitary gland, which releases hormones.",tip:"The pituitary is the link to the endocrine system."}],
["C3.1","Distinguish",2,"Distinguish between a voluntary action and a reflex action.","A voluntary action is a deliberate decision controlled by the brain's cortex|cortex|brain|decision|deliberate|chooses","A reflex is a rapid, automatic response that does not involve conscious thought, often via the spinal cord|automatic|involuntary|unconscious|spinal|rapid",{model:"A voluntary action is a deliberate decision controlled by the cerebral cortex. A reflex is a rapid, automatic response that bypasses conscious thought, often through the spinal cord.",tip:"Contrast control: decided vs automatic."}],
["C3.2","Outline",2,"Outline the role of phagocytes in defence against disease.","They engulf pathogens|engulf|engulfs|ingest|surround","And digest them with enzymes from lysosomes|digest|digests|lysosomes|enzymes|destroy",{model:"Phagocytes engulf pathogens into a vesicle and digest them with enzymes from lysosomes.",tip:"Engulf, then digest: two marks."}],
["C3.2","Distinguish",2,"Distinguish between antigens and antibodies.","Antigens are molecules on a pathogen's surface that trigger an immune response|surface|trigger|foreign|recognised","Antibodies are proteins made by plasma cells (B cells) that bind to specific antigens|proteins|plasma cells|b cells|lymphocytes|bind",{model:"Antigens are molecules on the surface of a pathogen that trigger an immune response. Antibodies are proteins made by plasma cells that bind to a specific antigen.",tip:"Say what each one is and where it comes from."}],
["C4.1","Outline",2,"Outline the shape of a sigmoid population growth curve.","Slow growth at first, then rapid, exponential growth|exponential|rapid|lag","Growth then slows as limiting factors act, levelling off at the carrying capacity|carrying capacity|levels off|plateau|limiting factors",{model:"A sigmoid curve starts with slow growth, then rapid exponential growth, then slows as limiting factors take effect, levelling off at the carrying capacity.",tip:"Name the three phases and the carrying capacity."}],
["C4.1","Outline",2,"Outline how random quadrat sampling estimates the population size of a plant.","Quadrats are placed at coordinates chosen from random numbers|coordinates|random numbers|grid|placed","The mean number per quadrat is multiplied up to the total area|mean|average|multiply|multiplied|total area|scale",{model:"Quadrats are placed at coordinates chosen using random numbers, the plants in each are counted, and the mean per quadrat is multiplied by the number of quadrats that fit in the whole area.",tip:"Say how positions are chosen and how the estimate is calculated."}],
["C4.2","Outline",2,"Outline why energy transfer between trophic levels is inefficient.","Energy is lost as heat from respiration|heat|respiration","Not all of an organism is eaten or digested; some is lost in faeces or dead remains|not eaten|faeces|feces|undigested|excretion|bones",{model:"Much of the energy is lost as heat from respiration, and not all of each organism is eaten or digested, so some energy is lost in faeces and dead remains.",tip:"Give two separate losses."}],
["C4.2","Outline",2,"Outline the role of decomposers in the carbon cycle.","They break down dead organic matter|break down|dead|rot","Releasing carbon dioxide into the air through respiration|carbon dioxide|co2|respiration|release",{model:"Decomposers break down dead organic matter and, by respiring, release the carbon in it back into the air as carbon dioxide.",tip:"Link breakdown to the CO2 released by respiration."}],
["D1.1","Outline",2,"Outline the role of DNA ligase in replication.","It joins the Okazaki fragments on the lagging strand|okazaki|lagging|fragments","By forming phosphodiester bonds in the sugar–phosphate backbone|phosphodiester|backbone|seals",{model:"DNA ligase joins the Okazaki fragments on the lagging strand by forming phosphodiester bonds, sealing the sugar–phosphate backbone.",tip:"Name the fragments and the bond."}],
["D1.1","Explain",2,"Explain why replication is continuous on one strand but discontinuous on the other.","DNA polymerase can only add nucleotides in the 5′ to 3′ direction|5 to 3|five to three|direction","The strands are antiparallel, so the lagging strand is made in short (Okazaki) fragments|antiparallel|okazaki|fragments",{model:"DNA polymerase only adds nucleotides in the 5′ to 3′ direction. Because the strands are antiparallel, one strand is made continuously, while the lagging strand is made in short Okazaki fragments.",tip:"Two ideas: the direction rule and antiparallel strands."}],
["D1.2","Outline",2,"Outline the role of tRNA in translation.","Each tRNA carries a specific amino acid to the ribosome|carries|brings|amino acid","Its anticodon pairs with the complementary codon on the mRNA|anticodon|codon|complementary",{model:"Each tRNA carries a specific amino acid to the ribosome, where its anticodon pairs with the complementary mRNA codon, so amino acids are added in the right order.",tip:"Codon and anticodon are the key terms."}],
["D1.2","Explain",2,"Explain why the genetic code is described as degenerate.","More than one codon can code for the same amino acid|more than one|same amino acid|several codons|multiple","Because there are 64 codons but only 20 amino acids|64|20",{model:"The code is degenerate because several codons can code for the same amino acid: there are 64 codons but only 20 amino acids.",tip:"Quote the numbers 64 and 20."}],
["D1.3","Distinguish",2,"Distinguish between a substitution mutation and a frameshift mutation.","A substitution replaces one base, changing at most one codon or amino acid|replaces|one base|single base|one codon|one amino acid","A frameshift (an insertion or deletion) shifts the reading frame, changing every codon after it|insertion|deletion|reading frame|every codon|all codons",{model:"A substitution replaces one base with another, so at most one codon and one amino acid change. A frameshift, caused by inserting or deleting a base, shifts the reading frame, changing every codon after it.",tip:"Compare how much of the protein is affected."}],
["D1.3","Outline",2,"Outline one cause of gene mutations.","A named mutagen such as UV light, X-rays or chemicals in tobacco smoke|uv|ultraviolet|x rays|radiation|chemicals|mutagen|mutagens|carcinogen","It damages DNA or changes its base sequence|damage|damages|alters|changes the base|base sequence",{model:"Mutagens such as ultraviolet radiation damage DNA and can change its base sequence, for example by causing errors when the DNA is copied.",tip:"Name the cause, then say what it does to DNA."}],
["D2.1","Outline",2,"Outline the role of crossing over in meiosis.","Homologous chromosomes exchange sections of chromatids at chiasmata in prophase I|exchange|swap|chiasma|chiasmata|homologous","Producing new combinations of alleles, which increases genetic variation|variation|new combinations|recombination|different combinations",{model:"In prophase I, homologous chromosomes exchange sections of their chromatids at chiasmata, producing new combinations of alleles and so more genetic variation.",tip:"Say when it happens and what it produces."}],
["D2.1","State",2,"State what happens during anaphase of mitosis.","Sister chromatids separate as the centromeres split|chromatids|separate|split","They are pulled to opposite poles by the spindle fibres|poles|spindle|opposite ends|pulled",{model:"In anaphase the centromeres split, the sister chromatids separate, and spindle fibres pull them to opposite poles of the cell.",tip:"Two marks: separation and movement to the poles."}],
["D2.2","Outline",2,"Outline how transcription factors regulate gene expression.","They are proteins that bind to the promoter or other specific DNA sequences|bind|binding|promoter|dna","They increase or decrease the rate at which RNA polymerase transcribes the gene|increase|decrease|activate|repress|rna polymerase|switch on",{model:"Transcription factors are proteins that bind to specific DNA sequences, such as the promoter, and increase or decrease the rate at which RNA polymerase transcribes the gene.",tip:"Where they bind and what they change."}],
["D2.2","Explain",2,"Explain how identical twins can develop different phenotypes.","They have the same DNA (genotype)|same dna|same genotype|same genes","But different environments cause different epigenetic changes, such as methylation, altering gene expression|environment|environments|epigenetic|methylation|lifestyle|diet",{model:"Identical twins have the same genotype, but different environments and lifestyles cause different epigenetic changes, such as DNA methylation, so different genes are expressed.",tip:"Genotype is the same; the difference is in gene expression."}],
["D2.3","Define",2,"Define water potential.","The potential energy of water, its tendency to move|potential energy|tendency|free energy","Water moves from higher to lower water potential|higher to lower|high to low|lower","It is measured in kPa, and pure water is 0|kpa|kilopascal|zero",{model:"Water potential is the potential energy of water, its tendency to move from one place to another. Water moves from higher to lower water potential; it is measured in kPa, and pure water is 0.",tip:"Include the direction water moves."}],
["D2.3","Outline",2,"Outline what happens to a plant cell placed in a hypertonic solution.","Water leaves the cell by osmosis|leaves|osmosis|out of the cell","The cytoplasm shrinks and the membrane pulls away from the cell wall (plasmolysis)|plasmolysis|plasmolysed|pulls away|shrinks|flaccid",{model:"Water leaves the cell by osmosis, so the cytoplasm shrinks and the membrane pulls away from the cell wall: the cell becomes plasmolysed.",tip:"Use the term plasmolysis."}],
["D3.1","Outline",2,"Outline the roles of FSH and LH in the menstrual cycle.","FSH stimulates the development of a follicle, which secretes oestrogen|follicle|follicles|oestrogen|estrogen","An LH surge triggers ovulation and the formation of the corpus luteum|ovulation|corpus luteum|surge",{model:"FSH stimulates a follicle to develop and secrete oestrogen. A surge in LH then triggers ovulation and turns the follicle into the corpus luteum.",tip:"One role per hormone."}],
["D3.1","Outline",2,"Outline two differences between male and female gametes in humans.","Sperm are small; eggs are large|small|large|size|larger","Sperm can swim using a flagellum; eggs cannot move by themselves|motile|flagellum|tail|swim","Eggs contain food reserves in their cytoplasm; sperm have very little|food|yolk|cytoplasm|nutrients","Millions of sperm are made but few eggs|millions|many|few|number",{model:"Sperm are tiny and swim using a flagellum, while eggs are large, cannot move by themselves and contain food reserves.",tip:"State both sides of each difference."}],
["D3.2","Outline",2,"Outline why sex-linked conditions such as haemophilia are more common in males.","The recessive allele is carried on the X chromosome|x chromosome|on the x|x linked","Males have only one X, so one recessive allele causes the condition, while females need two copies|only one|one x|single x|xy|two copies|need two",{model:"The recessive allele is on the X chromosome. Males have only one X, so one copy of the allele causes haemophilia, while females need two copies and are usually carriers.",tip:"Explain the difference in X chromosomes between the sexes."}],
["D3.2","Define",2,"Define the term allele.","One of the different forms of a gene|version|form|variant","Found at the same locus; alleles differ in their base sequence|locus|same position|base sequence|differ",{model:"An allele is one of the different forms of a gene, found at the same locus on a chromosome and differing slightly in base sequence.",tip:"Don't confuse allele with gene."}],
["D3.3","Outline",2,"Outline how the body responds when core temperature rises.","Skin arterioles dilate (vasodilation), increasing heat loss|vasodilation|dilate|dilation|blood flow","Sweat is released and cools the skin by evaporation|sweat|sweating|evaporation","Hair erector muscles relax so hairs lie flat|flat|relax",{model:"Arterioles in the skin dilate, so more blood flows near the surface and loses heat, and sweat glands release sweat that cools the skin as it evaporates.",tip:"Pick two responses and say how each loses heat."}],
["D3.3","Explain",2,"Explain the role of negative feedback in homeostasis.","A change from the set point is detected by receptors|set point|detected|receptors|deviation","A response then reverses the change, returning the value to the set point|reverses|opposes|returns|counteracts|corrects",{model:"When a variable moves away from its set point, receptors detect the change, and effectors bring about a response that reverses it, returning the value to the set point.",tip:"Use 'set point' and 'reverses the change'."}],
["D4.1","Outline",2,"Outline why variation is necessary for natural selection.","Individuals with favourable variations are more likely to survive and reproduce|survive|reproduce|favourable|advantage|fitter","They pass on their alleles, so allele frequencies change over generations|pass on|alleles|offspring|frequency|frequencies",{model:"Without variation there is nothing to select. Individuals with favourable variations survive and reproduce more, passing on their alleles, so allele frequencies change over generations.",tip:"Natural selection acts on existing variation; make that explicit."}],
["D4.1","Distinguish",2,"Distinguish between directional and stabilizing selection.","Directional selection favours one extreme phenotype, shifting the mean|one extreme|extreme|shifts|shift","Stabilizing selection favours the intermediate phenotype, reducing variation|intermediate|average|middle|reduces variation|narrows",{model:"Directional selection favours one extreme phenotype, so the mean shifts in that direction. Stabilizing selection favours the intermediate phenotype, reducing variation around the mean.",tip:"Describe what happens to the mean and the spread."}],
["D4.2","Outline",2,"Outline how eutrophication can harm aquatic ecosystems.","Excess nutrients such as nitrates from fertilisers cause algal blooms|nutrients|nitrates|phosphates|fertiliser|fertilizer|algal bloom|algae","Bacteria decomposing the dead algae use up oxygen, so fish and other animals die|oxygen|decomposers|bacteria|fish die|die",{model:"Fertiliser run-off adds nitrates and phosphates, causing algal blooms. When the algae die, bacteria decompose them and use up the oxygen, so fish and other animals die.",tip:"Follow the chain: nutrients → algae → decomposition → low oxygen."}],
["D4.2","Outline",2,"Outline two features of a stable ecosystem.","A supply of energy, usually sunlight|sunlight|sun|energy supply","Nutrients are recycled|recycling|recycled|nutrient cycling","High biodiversity and genetic diversity|biodiversity|diversity","Climate variables stay within the tolerance of its species|climate|tolerance",{model:"A stable ecosystem has a constant supply of energy from sunlight, and its nutrients are recycled by decomposers.",tip:"Two separate features, one sentence each."}],
["D4.3","Outline",2,"Outline how the melting of polar ice can cause further warming.","Ice reflects sunlight (high albedo), while open water absorbs more|albedo|reflects|reflect|absorbs|absorb","So more heat is absorbed, which melts more ice, and the cycle continues|more ice|melts more|more melting|cycle|continues",{model:"Ice reflects most sunlight (high albedo). As it melts, darker ocean absorbs more heat, which warms the water and melts more ice, so the warming feeds itself.",tip:"Albedo is the key term; then show the loop."}],
["D4.3","Outline",2,"Outline how carbon sequestration helps reduce global warming.","Carbon dioxide is captured and stored, e.g. by planting forests or storing it underground|forests|trees|planting|underground|stored|captured","So less carbon dioxide stays in the air to add to the greenhouse effect|removed|greenhouse|less carbon dioxide|reduces",{model:"Carbon sequestration captures carbon dioxide and stores it, for example in new forests or rock underground, so less of it stays in the air to enhance the greenhouse effect.",tip:"Give an example of how carbon is stored."}],
]);

/* ---------- a third set: every topic now has at least eight questions ---------- */
QB("bio", [
["A1.1","Outline",2,"Outline why water has a high latent heat of vaporization and why this matters to organisms.","Many hydrogen bonds must be broken for water molecules to evaporate|hydrogen bonds|break bonds|broken","So evaporating sweat or transpiration removes a lot of heat, cooling the organism|sweat|sweating|cooling|cools|transpiration|removes heat",{model:"Many hydrogen bonds must be broken for water to evaporate, so evaporation absorbs a lot of energy. Sweating and transpiration therefore remove a lot of heat, cooling the organism.",tip:"Cause (hydrogen bonds) then biological consequence."}],
["A1.1","Explain",2,"Explain how adhesion helps water move through plants.","Water forms hydrogen bonds with the hydrophilic cellulose walls of xylem vessels|cellulose|xylem walls|walls|hydrophilic|polar surfaces","This helps water rise against gravity by capillary action|capillary|against gravity|rise|climb",{model:"Water forms hydrogen bonds with the hydrophilic cellulose walls of xylem vessels, which helps it rise against gravity by capillary action.",tip:"Adhesion is water sticking to other substances; cohesion is water sticking to water."}],
["A1.1","Explain",2,"Explain why water is described as a polar molecule.","Oxygen is more electronegative than hydrogen, so electrons are shared unequally|electronegative|unequal|unequally|pulls electrons","Oxygen carries a partial negative charge and each hydrogen a partial positive charge|partial negative|partial positive|slightly negative|slightly positive|δ",{model:"Oxygen is more electronegative than hydrogen, so the shared electrons are pulled towards oxygen. Oxygen carries a partial negative charge and each hydrogen a partial positive charge.",tip:"Use δ+ and δ− correctly."}],
["A1.1","Outline",2,"Outline why water is a good medium for metabolic reactions.","Many substances dissolve in it, so reactants can meet and react|dissolve|solvent|dissolved","Its high heat capacity keeps temperature stable, so enzymes work efficiently|stable temperature|heat capacity|enzymes|optimum",{model:"Many substances dissolve in water, so reactants can move and collide, and water's high heat capacity keeps temperatures stable for enzymes.",tip:"Link each property to its biological benefit."}],
["A1.2","Outline",2,"Outline how the structure of DNA allows it to be copied accurately.","The two strands are complementary: A pairs with T and C with G|complementary|a with t|base pairing|a pairs with t","So each strand acts as a template for a new strand|template|each strand|new strand",{model:"The two strands are complementary (A–T, C–G), so each strand can act as a template for building an identical new strand.",tip:"Complementary pairing is the key idea."}],
["A1.2","State",2,"State the sugar found in RNA and one base found only in RNA.","Ribose|ribose","Uracil|uracil",{model:"Ribose, and uracil.",tip:"Uracil replaces thymine in RNA."}],
["A1.2","Outline",2,"Outline what is meant by the antiparallel arrangement of DNA strands.","The two strands run in opposite directions|opposite directions|opposite","One runs 5′ to 3′ and the other 3′ to 5′|5 to 3|3 to 5|5 3|3 5",{model:"The two strands run in opposite directions: one 5′ to 3′ and the other 3′ to 5′.",tip:"Refer to the 5′ and 3′ ends."}],
["A1.2","Outline",2,"Outline the role of hydrogen bonds in the DNA double helix.","They hold the complementary base pairs together between the two strands|base pairs|hold the strands|between bases|complementary","Each is weak, so the strands can be separated for replication and transcription|weak|separated|unzipped|replication|transcription",{model:"Hydrogen bonds hold complementary base pairs together between the strands. Each is weak, so the strands can be separated for replication and transcription.",tip:"Strength in numbers, weakness individually."}],
["A2.1","Outline",2,"Outline the conditions on the early Earth that allowed organic molecules to form.","An atmosphere with no oxygen, containing gases such as methane, ammonia and hydrogen|no oxygen|reducing|methane|ammonia","Energy from lightning, UV radiation or volcanic heat|lightning|uv|ultraviolet|volcanic|energy",{model:"The early atmosphere had no oxygen and contained methane, ammonia and hydrogen, and lightning and UV radiation supplied the energy for reactions.",tip:"Gases plus an energy source."}],
["A2.1","State",2,"State two features shared by all living cells.","A cell membrane|membrane","DNA as genetic material|dna|genetic material","Ribosomes|ribosomes","Cytoplasm|cytoplasm",{model:"A cell membrane and ribosomes.",tip:"Universal features suggest a common origin."}],
["A2.1","Outline",2,"Outline the role of membranes in the origin of the first cells.","Phospholipids or fatty acids naturally assemble into vesicles in water|vesicles|assemble|spontaneously|bilayer|phospholipids|fatty acids","Enclosing molecules so a separate internal chemistry could develop|separate|enclose|internal|compartment|concentrate",{model:"Phospholipids and fatty acids naturally assemble into vesicles in water, enclosing molecules so that a separate internal chemistry could develop.",tip:"Self-assembly is the key idea."}],
["A2.1","Outline",2,"Outline what is meant by LUCA.","The last universal common ancestor|last universal common ancestor|common ancestor","From which all living organisms are descended|all living|all organisms|descended|evolved from",{model:"LUCA is the last universal common ancestor, from which all living organisms on Earth are descended.",tip:"Evidence: the universal genetic code."}],
["A2.2","State",2,"State the formula for magnification and what each term means.","Magnification = image size ÷ actual size|image size|image","The actual size is the real size of the specimen|actual size|real size|specimen",{model:"Magnification = image size ÷ actual size, where the actual size is the real size of the specimen.",tip:"Use the same units for both sizes."}],
["A2.2","Outline",2,"Outline the function of the nucleolus.","Makes ribosomal RNA (rRNA)|rrna|ribosomal rna","Where ribosome subunits are assembled|ribosomes|assembled|ribosome subunits",{model:"The nucleolus makes ribosomal RNA and assembles ribosome subunits.",tip:"It is inside the nucleus but has no membrane."}],
["A2.2","Outline",2,"Outline one advantage of light microscopes over electron microscopes.","Living cells can be viewed|living|alive","In colour, cheaply and with simple preparation|colour|color|cheap|cheaper|simple preparation|portable",{model:"Light microscopes can show living cells, in colour, and are cheap and simple to use.",tip:"Electron microscopes need dead, prepared specimens."}],
["A2.2","Outline",3,"Outline the structure of a prokaryotic cell.","A cell wall made of peptidoglycan|peptidoglycan|cell wall","Naked circular DNA in the nucleoid, plus plasmids|circular dna|nucleoid|plasmids|naked","70S ribosomes in the cytoplasm and no membrane-bound organelles|70s|no membrane bound|no organelles|no nucleus",{model:"A prokaryotic cell has a peptidoglycan cell wall, naked circular DNA in a nucleoid region plus plasmids, and 70S ribosomes, with no membrane-bound organelles.",tip:"Name specific structures."}],
["A2.3","Outline",2,"Outline how viruses show diversity.","Their genetic material can be DNA or RNA, single- or double-stranded|dna or rna|single stranded|double stranded|rna viruses","Some have a lipid envelope, and their shapes and hosts vary|envelope|enveloped|shapes|bacteriophage|hosts",{model:"Viruses may have DNA or RNA, single- or double-stranded; some have a lipid envelope, and their shapes and host cells vary widely.",tip:"Variety suggests viruses may have several origins."}],
["A2.3","Explain",2,"Explain why RNA viruses such as influenza evolve rapidly.","RNA copying has no proofreading, so mutation rates are high|no proofreading|mutation rate|mutations|errors","Short generation times and huge numbers mean new variants arise and are selected quickly|generation|large numbers|selection|selected|new strains|variants",{model:"RNA replication has no proofreading, so mutations are frequent, and with short generation times and huge numbers, new variants arise and are selected very quickly.",tip:"Mutation plus selection."}],
["A2.3","Outline",3,"Outline the lysogenic cycle.","Viral DNA is inserted into the host chromosome|inserted|integrated|integrates|host chromosome|host dna","It is copied each time the host cell divides|copied|replicated|divides|cell division","Later it can leave the chromosome and enter the lytic cycle|lytic|leaves|excised|triggered",{model:"Viral DNA is integrated into the host chromosome and copied whenever the host cell divides. Later it can leave the chromosome and enter the lytic cycle.",tip:"The virus is dormant but copied."}],
["A2.3","State",2,"State two ways HIV is transmitted.","Sexual contact|sex|sexual","Shared needles or blood transfusions|needles|blood|transfusion","From mother to baby at birth or in breast milk|mother|baby|breastfeeding|birth",{model:"Through sexual contact and through shared needles.",tip:"HIV spreads in body fluids, not by touch."}],
["A3.1","State",2,"State, in order, the ranks between kingdom and species in the classification hierarchy.","Phylum, class, order|phylum|class order","Family, genus|family|genus",{model:"Phylum, class, order, family, genus.",tip:"King Philip Came Over For Good Soup."}],
["A3.1","Define",2,"Define karyotype.","The number and appearance of the chromosomes in a cell|number and appearance|chromosomes|number of chromosomes","Arranged in homologous pairs by size, e.g. to detect trisomy 21|pairs|homologous|by size|trisomy|down syndrome|abnormalities",{model:"A karyotype is the number and appearance of the chromosomes in a cell, arranged in homologous pairs by size; it can reveal abnormalities such as trisomy 21.",tip:"Distinguish the karyotype from the karyogram image."}],
["A3.1","Outline",2,"Outline how DNA barcoding is used to identify species.","A short, standard DNA sequence is read from the sample|short sequence|standard sequence|sequenced|gene region|cox1","And compared with a database of known species|compared|database|matched|reference",{model:"A short, standard section of DNA is sequenced from the sample and compared with a reference database of known species to identify it.",tip:"Useful when organisms look alike."}],
["A3.1","Explain",2,"Explain why the biological species concept can't be applied to organisms that reproduce asexually.","The concept is based on interbreeding to produce fertile offspring|interbreeding|interbreed|fertile offspring","Asexual organisms such as bacteria don't interbreed, so the test can't be applied|don t interbreed|no interbreeding|bacteria|clones|can t be tested",{model:"The biological species concept depends on interbreeding to produce fertile offspring. Asexual organisms such as bacteria don't interbreed, so the test can't be used.",tip:"Name an example."}],
["A3.2","Outline",2,"Outline the principle of parsimony in cladistics.","The simplest cladogram is preferred|simplest|fewest|least","The one requiring the fewest evolutionary changes|evolutionary changes|changes|steps|mutations",{model:"Parsimony means choosing the cladogram that needs the fewest evolutionary changes, as the simplest explanation is most likely.",tip:"Simplest explanation wins."}],
["A3.2","Outline",2,"Outline why the classification of organisms is sometimes revised.","New evidence, especially DNA sequences, reveals different relationships|dna|sequences|molecular|new evidence","Groups are changed to reflect true common ancestry|common ancestry|clades|evolutionary relationships|figwort",{model:"New evidence, especially DNA sequences, can reveal different evolutionary relationships, so groups are reorganised to reflect true common ancestry, as with the figwort family.",tip:"Figworts are the classic example."}],
["A3.2","Outline",2,"Outline what the nodes and branches of a cladogram represent.","Nodes represent common ancestors where lineages split|common ancestor|ancestor|split|divergence","Branch lengths or the number of differences can represent time since divergence|time|differences|mutations|molecular clock",{model:"Each node represents a common ancestor where lineages split, and branch lengths can represent the time since divergence, based on the number of differences.",tip:"Nodes are hypothetical ancestors."}],
["A3.2","Outline",2,"Outline why the three-domain system replaced the five-kingdom system.","rRNA sequencing showed archaea differ from bacteria as much as from eukaryotes|archaea|rrna|ribosomal rna|woese","So prokaryotes were split into two domains, Bacteria and Archaea|bacteria and archaea|split|two domains|prokaryotes",{model:"Woese's rRNA sequencing showed that archaea are as different from bacteria as from eukaryotes, so prokaryotes were split into two separate domains.",tip:"Name Woese and rRNA."}],
["A4.1","Outline",2,"Outline how homologous structures provide evidence for evolution.","Different species share the same basic structure, e.g. the pentadactyl limb|pentadactyl|same basic|same structure|limb","This suggests descent from a common ancestor, adapted for different functions|common ancestor|different functions|adaptive radiation|divergent",{model:"Different species share the same basic structure, such as the pentadactyl limb, used for different functions; this suggests they descended from a common ancestor.",tip:"Same structure, different function."}],
["A4.1","Outline",2,"Outline how polyploidy can lead to speciation.","Errors in meiosis produce offspring with extra whole sets of chromosomes|extra sets|whole sets|extra chromosomes|non disjunction","These can't produce fertile offspring with the parent population, so they are reproductively isolated|reproductively isolated|isolated|can t interbreed|infertile|sympatric",{model:"Errors in meiosis can produce offspring with extra whole sets of chromosomes. They can't produce fertile offspring with the parent population, so they are immediately reproductively isolated.",tip:"Common in plants."}],
["A4.1","Distinguish",2,"Distinguish between convergent and divergent evolution.","Convergent: unrelated species evolve similar features (analogous structures)|unrelated|analogous|similar features|similar environments","Divergent: related species become different as they adapt to different environments (homologous structures)|related|homologous|different environments|become different",{model:"In convergent evolution unrelated species evolve similar, analogous features. In divergent evolution related species become different, giving homologous structures.",tip:"Pair each with the type of structure."}],
["A4.1","Outline",2,"Outline how fossils provide evidence for evolution.","Fossils in successive rock layers show organisms changing over time|rock layers|strata|changing over time|older layers","Transitional fossils show intermediate forms, e.g. Archaeopteryx|transitional|intermediate|archaeopteryx|tiktaalik",{model:"Fossils in successive rock layers show organisms changing over time, and transitional fossils such as Archaeopteryx show intermediate forms.",tip:"Name a transitional fossil."}],
["A4.2","Outline",2,"Outline why invasive species threaten biodiversity.","They have no natural predators in the new area, so their populations grow rapidly|no predators|no natural predators|grow rapidly|spread","They outcompete, prey on or spread disease to native species|outcompete|compete|prey|disease|native",{model:"Invasive species often have no natural predators in their new area, so they spread rapidly and outcompete, eat or infect native species.",tip:"Give a named example, e.g. cane toads."}],
["A4.2","Outline",2,"Outline how seed banks help conserve biodiversity.","Seeds of many species and varieties are stored in cold, dry conditions|stored|cold|dry|frozen","Preserving genetic diversity so plants can be regrown after extinction or disaster|genetic diversity|regrown|replanted|extinction|backup|svalbard",{model:"Seed banks store seeds of many species in cold, dry conditions, preserving genetic diversity so plants can be regrown if they die out in the wild.",tip:"This is ex situ conservation."}],
["A4.2","Explain",2,"Explain why biodiversity hotspots are a priority for conservation.","They contain very high numbers of endemic species|endemic|high numbers|many species|unique","And they face high levels of habitat loss|habitat loss|threatened|lost|threat|destroyed",{model:"Hotspots contain very large numbers of endemic species found nowhere else, and they face severe habitat loss, so protecting them saves the most species.",tip:"Endemism plus threat."}],
["A4.2","Outline",2,"Outline one reason for conserving biodiversity.","Ecosystem services such as pollination, clean water and climate regulation|pollination|ecosystem services|clean water|climate","Or economic value from medicines, food and tourism, or ethical reasons|medicines|food|tourism|ethical|intrinsic value",{model:"Biodiversity provides ecosystem services such as pollination and clean water, as well as medicines and food.",tip:"Ecological, economic or ethical reasons all count."}],
["B1.1","Distinguish",2,"Distinguish between alpha glucose and beta glucose.","In alpha glucose the –OH on carbon 1 is below the ring|below the ring|below|points down","In beta glucose the –OH on carbon 1 is above the ring|above the ring|above|points up",{model:"In α-glucose the hydroxyl on carbon 1 is below the ring; in β-glucose it is above the ring.",tip:"Refer to carbon 1."}],
["B1.1","Outline",2,"Outline how glycogen's structure suits its function.","It is highly branched, so glucose can be released quickly from many ends|branched|many ends|quickly|rapidly","It is insoluble and compact, so it doesn't affect osmosis and stores a lot in a small space|insoluble|compact|osmosis|small space",{model:"Glycogen is highly branched, so enzymes can release glucose quickly from many ends, and it is insoluble and compact, so it doesn't affect osmosis.",tip:"Structure → function."}],
["B1.1","Outline",2,"Outline the structure of a phospholipid.","A glycerol attached to two fatty acids and a phosphate group|glycerol|two fatty acids|fatty acids","With a hydrophilic head and two hydrophobic tails|hydrophilic|hydrophobic|head|tails",{model:"A phospholipid is a glycerol bonded to two fatty acids and a phosphate group, giving a hydrophilic head and two hydrophobic tails.",tip:"Amphipathic: one end loves water, one hates it."}],
["B1.1","Outline",2,"Outline the role of cellulose in plants.","It forms the cell wall|cell wall|walls","Its chains are linked by hydrogen bonds into strong microfibrils that resist turgor pressure|microfibrils|hydrogen bonds|strength|strong|support|turgor",{model:"Cellulose forms the plant cell wall: its chains are linked by hydrogen bonds into strong microfibrils that resist turgor pressure and support the plant.",tip:"β-glucose chains, alternate units flipped."}],
["B1.2","Outline",2,"Outline the tertiary structure of a protein.","The overall 3D folding of a polypeptide|3d|three dimensional|folding|folded","Held by bonds between R groups: hydrogen, ionic and disulfide bonds, and hydrophobic interactions|r groups|disulfide|ionic|hydrophobic|hydrogen bonds",{model:"Tertiary structure is the 3D folding of a polypeptide, held by hydrogen, ionic and disulfide bonds and hydrophobic interactions between R groups.",tip:"Name at least two bond types."}],
["B1.2","Distinguish",2,"Distinguish between fibrous and globular proteins.","Fibrous proteins are long, insoluble and structural, e.g. collagen|long|insoluble|structural|collagen|keratin","Globular proteins are rounded, soluble and functional, e.g. enzymes|rounded|spherical|soluble|enzymes|haemoglobin|hemoglobin",{model:"Fibrous proteins are long, insoluble and structural, like collagen. Globular proteins are rounded, soluble and functional, like enzymes.",tip:"Give an example of each."}],
["B1.2","Outline",2,"Outline why proteins are so diverse.","There are 20 amino acids that can be joined in any order|20 amino acids|twenty|any order|sequence","So an enormous number of different sequences, and therefore shapes, is possible|enormous|huge number|different shapes|combinations",{model:"There are 20 amino acids that can be joined in any sequence, so an enormous number of different polypeptides, with different shapes, is possible.",tip:"20ⁿ possible sequences."}],
["B1.2","Outline",2,"Outline the quaternary structure of haemoglobin.","Four polypeptide chains (two alpha and two beta)|four|4|polypeptides|chains","Each with a haem group containing iron that binds oxygen|haem|heme|iron|binds oxygen",{model:"Haemoglobin has four polypeptide chains, two alpha and two beta, each with a haem group containing iron that binds oxygen.",tip:"Quaternary = more than one polypeptide."}],
["B2.1","Outline",2,"Outline the fluid mosaic model of membrane structure.","A phospholipid bilayer in which the molecules can move|bilayer|phospholipids move|molecules move","With proteins scattered through it|proteins|scattered|embedded",{model:"The membrane is a phospholipid bilayer whose molecules can move, with proteins scattered through it like tiles in a mosaic.",tip:"Explain both 'fluid' and 'mosaic'."}],
["B2.1","Outline",2,"Outline how endocytosis takes substances into a cell.","The membrane folds inwards around the material|folds inwards|engulfs|surrounds|invaginates","And pinches off to form a vesicle, using ATP|vesicle|pinches off|atp",{model:"In endocytosis the membrane folds inwards around the material and pinches off to form a vesicle inside the cell, using ATP.",tip:"It depends on membrane fluidity."}],
["B2.1","Outline",2,"Outline the role of channel proteins.","They form hydrophilic pores through the membrane|pores|pore|hydrophilic|tunnel","Allowing specific ions to diffuse passively down their gradient; some are gated|specific ions|ions|passive|facilitated diffusion|gated|concentration gradient",{model:"Channel proteins form hydrophilic pores through the membrane that let specific ions diffuse passively down their concentration gradient; some open or close (gated).",tip:"Channels are passive."}],
["B2.1","Explain",2,"Explain why the sodium–potassium pump is an example of active transport.","It moves ions against their concentration gradients: 3 Na⁺ out and 2 K⁺ in|against|3 na|3 sodium|2 k|2 potassium|concentration gradient","Using energy from ATP|atp|energy",{model:"The pump moves 3 Na⁺ out and 2 K⁺ in against their concentration gradients, using energy from ATP, so it is active transport.",tip:"Against the gradient + ATP."}],
["B2.2","Outline",2,"Outline the function of lysosomes.","They contain hydrolytic (digestive) enzymes|hydrolytic|digestive enzymes|enzymes","Which break down worn-out organelles and engulfed material|break down|digest|worn out|engulfed|bacteria|autolysis",{model:"Lysosomes contain hydrolytic enzymes that break down worn-out organelles and material taken in by phagocytosis.",tip:"The membrane keeps the enzymes away from the cytoplasm."}],
["B2.2","Outline",2,"Outline how the structure of a mitochondrion suits its function.","The inner membrane is folded into cristae, giving a large surface area for the electron transport chain|cristae|folded|surface area|electron transport","The matrix contains the enzymes of the Krebs cycle|matrix|krebs|enzymes|link reaction",{model:"The inner membrane is folded into cristae, giving a large area for the electron transport chain, and the matrix contains the Krebs cycle enzymes.",tip:"Structure → function for each part."}],
["B2.2","Outline",3,"Outline the route a secreted protein takes through the cell.","It is made on ribosomes on the rough ER|rough er|ribosomes|rer","It is carried in vesicles to the Golgi apparatus, where it is modified|golgi|modified|vesicles","It is packaged into vesicles that fuse with the cell membrane (exocytosis)|exocytosis|fuse|membrane",{model:"The protein is made on ribosomes of the rough ER, carried in vesicles to the Golgi to be modified, then packaged into vesicles that fuse with the membrane and release it by exocytosis.",tip:"RER → Golgi → membrane."}],
["B2.2","Outline",2,"Outline the structure of a chloroplast.","A double membrane enclosing the stroma|double membrane|stroma","Containing stacks of thylakoids (grana) that hold chlorophyll|thylakoids|grana|granum|chlorophyll",{model:"A chloroplast has a double membrane enclosing the stroma, which contains stacks of thylakoids (grana) holding chlorophyll.",tip:"Thylakoids: light reactions; stroma: Calvin cycle."}],
["B2.3","Distinguish",3,"Distinguish between totipotent, pluripotent and multipotent stem cells.","Totipotent cells can form any cell type, including the placenta, e.g. the zygote|zygote|placenta|any cell including","Pluripotent cells can form any body cell type, e.g. embryonic stem cells|embryonic|any body cell|any type of body cell","Multipotent cells can form only a few related cell types, e.g. bone marrow|few|related|limited|bone marrow|adult",{model:"Totipotent cells (the zygote) can form any cell, including the placenta; pluripotent cells (embryonic) can form any body cell; multipotent cells (bone marrow) form only a few related types.",tip:"Give an example of each."}],
["B2.3","Outline",2,"Outline how a neuron is adapted to its function.","A long axon carries impulses over long distances|long axon|axon|long","A myelin sheath speeds conduction, and dendrites connect with many other cells|myelin|dendrites|many connections|speed",{model:"A neuron has a long axon to carry impulses long distances, a myelin sheath to speed conduction, and many dendrites to connect with other cells.",tip:"Feature → function."}],
["B2.3","Outline",2,"Outline one ethical issue with using embryonic stem cells.","The embryo is destroyed to obtain the cells|destroyed|destroying|killed|embryo dies","Some believe an embryo has a right to life from conception|right to life|potential life|conception|moral status|religious",{model:"Obtaining embryonic stem cells destroys the embryo, and some people believe an embryo has a right to life from conception.",tip:"State the issue and the belief behind it."}],
["B2.3","Explain",2,"Explain why larger organisms need specialized exchange surfaces.","As size increases, the surface area to volume ratio decreases|surface area to volume|sa v|ratio decreases|smaller ratio","So diffusion across the body surface is too slow to supply all cells|diffusion|too slow|insufficient|not enough|distance",{model:"As organisms get larger their surface area to volume ratio falls, so diffusion across the body surface can't supply all their cells fast enough.",tip:"SA:V ratio is the key."}],
["B3.1","Outline",2,"Outline the process of exhalation at rest.","The diaphragm and external intercostal muscles relax|relax|relaxes","Lung volume decreases, so pressure rises above atmospheric and air is pushed out|volume decreases|pressure rises|pressure increases|pushed out|forced out",{model:"At rest the diaphragm and external intercostals relax, so the thorax volume decreases, pressure rises above atmospheric pressure and air is pushed out.",tip:"Exhalation at rest is passive."}],
["B3.1","Outline",2,"Outline how stomata regulate gas exchange and water loss.","Guard cells become turgid and open the stomata, letting carbon dioxide in|guard cells|turgid|open|co2 in","In drought they become flaccid and close the stomata, reducing water loss|close|closed|flaccid|reduce water loss|drought",{model:"When guard cells are turgid the stomata open and CO₂ enters; in drought they become flaccid and close the stomata, reducing water loss.",tip:"There is a trade-off between CO₂ uptake and water loss."}],
["B3.1","Explain",2,"Explain how a steep concentration gradient is maintained at the alveoli.","Ventilation brings in fresh air with a high oxygen concentration|ventilation|breathing|fresh air","Blood flow carries oxygen away continuously|blood flow|carried away|circulation|capillaries",{model:"Ventilation keeps bringing in fresh air rich in oxygen, and blood flow continuously carries oxygen away, keeping the gradient steep.",tip:"Two processes, one at each side."}],
["B3.1","Define",2,"Define tidal volume and vital capacity.","Tidal volume is the volume of air breathed in or out in a normal breath|normal breath|each breath|at rest|normal breathing","Vital capacity is the maximum volume exhaled after a maximum inhalation|maximum|deepest|largest|forced",{model:"Tidal volume is the volume of air breathed in or out in a normal breath. Vital capacity is the maximum volume that can be exhaled after the deepest possible breath in.",tip:"Normal versus maximum."}],
["B3.2","Outline",3,"Outline the cardiac cycle.","The atria contract (atrial systole), pushing blood into the ventricles|atria contract|atrial systole","The ventricles contract (ventricular systole): AV valves close and blood is pumped into the arteries|ventricles contract|ventricular systole|av valves close|arteries","The heart relaxes (diastole) and the chambers fill with blood|diastole|relaxes|fills",{model:"The atria contract, pushing blood into the ventricles; the ventricles then contract, closing the AV valves and pumping blood into the arteries; finally the heart relaxes in diastole and fills with blood.",tip:"Include the valves."}],
["B3.2","Outline",2,"Outline the role of the sinoatrial (SA) node.","It is the heart's natural pacemaker in the wall of the right atrium|pacemaker|right atrium","It sends out electrical impulses that start each heartbeat|electrical|impulses|starts each|initiates|contraction",{model:"The SA node, in the right atrium, is the heart's natural pacemaker: it sends out electrical impulses that start each heartbeat.",tip:"The heart is myogenic."}],
["B3.2","Outline",2,"Outline how roots absorb water.","Root hairs give a large surface area|root hairs|surface area","Water enters by osmosis because active uptake of minerals lowers the water potential of root cells|osmosis|water potential|minerals|active transport|solutes",{model:"Root hairs give a large surface area, and water enters by osmosis because the active uptake of minerals lowers the water potential inside root cells.",tip:"Minerals are taken up actively; water follows passively."}],
["B3.2","Define",2,"Define transpiration and give one factor that increases its rate.","The loss of water vapour from leaves, mainly through stomata|water vapour|evaporation|stomata|from leaves","A factor such as higher temperature, wind, light or low humidity|temperature|wind|light|humidity|dry",{model:"Transpiration is the loss of water vapour from leaves, mainly through stomata. A higher temperature increases its rate.",tip:"Explain the direction of the effect if asked."}],
["B3.3","Outline",2,"Outline the structure of a sarcomere.","The section of a myofibril between two Z lines|z lines|z line|between","Containing overlapping thin actin and thick myosin filaments|actin|myosin|filaments|overlap",{model:"A sarcomere is the section of a myofibril between two Z lines, containing overlapping thin actin and thick myosin filaments.",tip:"It shortens during contraction."}],
["B3.3","Outline",2,"Outline the roles of tendons and ligaments.","Tendons attach muscles to bones|attach muscle|muscle to bone","Ligaments connect bone to bone at joints and stabilize them|bone to bone|stabilize|stabilise|joints",{model:"Tendons attach muscles to bones, transmitting the force of contraction. Ligaments join bone to bone and stabilize joints.",tip:"Tendons: muscle–bone; ligaments: bone–bone."}],
["B3.3","Outline",2,"Outline the role of synovial fluid.","It lubricates the joint|lubricates|lubrication|lubricant","Reducing friction between the cartilage-covered ends of the bones|friction|cartilage|smooth movement|wear",{model:"Synovial fluid lubricates the joint, reducing friction between the cartilage-covered ends of the bones.",tip:"Lubrication reduces friction."}],
["B3.3","Outline",2,"Outline why animals need to move.","To find food, water or a mate|food|mate|water|reproduction","To escape predators or unfavourable conditions, e.g. by migration|escape|predators|migration|conditions",{model:"Animals move to find food and mates, and to escape predators or poor conditions, for example by migrating.",tip:"Give two distinct reasons."}],
["B4.1","Outline",2,"Outline how mangrove trees are adapted to their habitat.","Salt glands or filtering roots cope with salt water|salt|seawater|excrete salt|filter","Aerial roots (pneumatophores) take in oxygen from the waterlogged mud|pneumatophores|aerial roots|oxygen|waterlogged|breathing roots",{model:"Mangroves cope with salt water using salt-excreting glands or filtering roots, and pneumatophores take in oxygen above the waterlogged mud.",tip:"Two problems: salt and lack of oxygen."}],
["B4.1","Define",2,"Define the term ecosystem.","A community of organisms|community|organisms|living","Interacting with each other and with their abiotic environment|abiotic|environment|non living|interacting",{model:"An ecosystem is a community of organisms interacting with each other and with their abiotic environment.",tip:"Both biotic and abiotic parts."}],
["B4.1","Outline",2,"Outline what is meant by a species' range of tolerance.","The range of an abiotic factor within which a species can survive|can survive|survives|within which|between an upper and lower limit|upper and lower","Outside this range it can't survive; it thrives best at the optimum|optimum|zone of stress|beyond the limits|dies beyond|can t survive beyond",{model:"A species can survive only within a certain range of an abiotic factor such as temperature; it thrives near the optimum and cannot survive outside the range.",tip:"Optimum, stress zones and limits."}],
["B4.1","Outline",2,"Outline how temperature and rainfall determine the distribution of biomes.","They set which plants can grow, e.g. tropical rainforest needs high temperature and rainfall|rainforest|plants|vegetation|desert|tundra","So biomes form in climate zones, as shown on a climograph|climate zones|climograph|latitude|similar climates",{model:"Temperature and rainfall determine which plants can grow, so biomes such as rainforest, desert and tundra occur in matching climate zones, as shown on a climograph.",tip:"Use a named biome."}],
["B4.2","Outline",2,"Outline what is meant by mixotrophic nutrition.","An organism that can feed both autotrophically and heterotrophically|both|autotrophic|heterotrophic|photosynthesis and","E.g. Euglena photosynthesises in light but feeds on organic matter in the dark|euglena|in the dark|in light|depending",{model:"A mixotroph can feed both autotrophically and heterotrophically; Euglena photosynthesises in light but absorbs organic matter in the dark.",tip:"Euglena is the standard example."}],
["B4.2","Outline",2,"Outline how saprotrophs obtain nutrients.","They secrete digestive enzymes onto dead organic matter|secrete|enzymes|dead|external digestion","And absorb the products of digestion|absorb|absorption",{model:"Saprotrophs secrete digestive enzymes onto dead organic matter and absorb the products.",tip:"Digestion happens outside the organism."}],
["B4.2","Outline",2,"Outline two adaptations of herbivores for feeding on plants.","Flat, grinding molars|grinding|flat molars|molars","A long gut or microbes to digest cellulose, e.g. a rumen|cellulose|rumen|bacteria|long gut|microbes","Incisors for cropping plants|incisors|cropping",{model:"Herbivores have flat molars for grinding plants and gut microbes, often in a rumen, to digest cellulose.",tip:"Teeth and digestion."}],
["B4.2","Outline",2,"Outline chemosynthesis.","Making organic compounds using energy from oxidizing inorganic chemicals|inorganic|oxidizing|oxidising|chemical energy","E.g. bacteria at hydrothermal vents oxidizing hydrogen sulfide|hydrothermal|vents|sulfide|sulphide|bacteria",{model:"Chemosynthesis is making organic compounds using energy released by oxidizing inorganic chemicals, as bacteria at hydrothermal vents do with hydrogen sulfide.",tip:"No light is needed."}],
["C1.1","Define",2,"Define activation energy.","The minimum energy needed to start a reaction|minimum energy|energy needed|start a reaction","Enzymes lower it, increasing the rate of reaction|lower|reduce|enzymes|catalyst",{model:"Activation energy is the minimum energy needed for a reaction to start; enzymes lower it, so reactions go faster.",tip:"Enzymes don't change the overall energy change."}],
["C1.1","Outline",2,"Outline end-product inhibition.","The end product of a pathway inhibits an enzyme early in the pathway|first enzyme|early|pathway|earlier enzyme","Usually by binding to an allosteric site, so the pathway slows when product builds up|allosteric|builds up|accumulates|negative feedback|slows",{model:"The final product of a metabolic pathway binds to the allosteric site of an enzyme early in the pathway, slowing the pathway when the product builds up.",tip:"It is negative feedback."}],
["C1.1","Explain",2,"Explain the effect of pH on enzyme activity.","Each enzyme has an optimum pH|optimum","Away from it, the charges on R groups change, altering the active site so the enzyme denatures|charges|ionic bonds|hydrogen bonds|active site|denature|denatures|denatured",{model:"Each enzyme has an optimum pH. Away from it, charges on R groups change, breaking ionic and hydrogen bonds and altering the active site, so the enzyme denatures.",tip:"Link pH to bonds and active-site shape."}],
["C1.1","Distinguish",2,"Distinguish between anabolism and catabolism.","Anabolism builds larger molecules from smaller ones, using energy|builds|synthesis|condensation|larger molecules","Catabolism breaks large molecules into smaller ones, releasing energy|breaks down|breakdown|hydrolysis|smaller molecules|releases energy",{model:"Anabolism builds large molecules from smaller ones, using energy, e.g. protein synthesis. Catabolism breaks large molecules down, releasing energy, e.g. respiration.",tip:"Build up versus break down."}],
]);

QB("bio", [
["C1.2","Outline",2,"Outline the link reaction.","Pyruvate enters the mitochondrion and is decarboxylated (CO₂ removed)|decarboxylated|co2 removed|carbon dioxide|loses co2","And oxidized, reducing NAD, to form acetyl CoA|acetyl coa|acetyl|nadh|reduced nad|oxidized|oxidised",{model:"Pyruvate enters the mitochondrial matrix, loses CO₂ (decarboxylation) and is oxidized, reducing NAD, to form acetyl CoA.",tip:"Decarboxylation + oxidation."}],
["C1.2","Outline",2,"Outline the role of ATP synthase in chemiosmosis.","Protons diffuse back into the matrix through ATP synthase, down their gradient|protons|h+|hydrogen ions|gradient","The flow of protons drives the phosphorylation of ADP to ATP|adp|phosphorylation|phosphorylates|drives",{model:"Protons diffuse back into the matrix through ATP synthase, down their concentration gradient, and this flow drives the phosphorylation of ADP to ATP.",tip:"Gradient → flow → ATP."}],
["C1.2","Explain",2,"Explain why anaerobic respiration yields much less ATP than aerobic respiration.","Only glycolysis takes place, giving a net 2 ATP per glucose|only glycolysis|glycolysis|2 atp|two atp","Without oxygen the Krebs cycle and electron transport chain stop, and they make most ATP|krebs|electron transport|oxidative phosphorylation|no oxygen|without oxygen",{model:"Anaerobic respiration uses only glycolysis, giving 2 ATP per glucose; without oxygen the Krebs cycle and electron transport chain, which make most ATP, can't run.",tip:"Compare 2 ATP with about 30."}],
["C1.2","Outline",2,"Outline how a respirometer measures the rate of respiration.","Organisms in a sealed container take up oxygen, and CO₂ is absorbed by soda lime or KOH|soda lime|koh|potassium hydroxide|absorbed|sealed","The fall in gas volume moves a liquid along a capillary tube, measured over time|capillary|liquid moves|bead|volume of oxygen|distance moved|over time",{model:"Organisms in a sealed tube take up oxygen while soda lime absorbs the CO₂ they release, so the gas volume falls and a liquid moves along a capillary tube; its distance over time gives the rate.",tip:"Why the CO₂ must be absorbed."}],
["C1.3","Outline",2,"Outline the role of photolysis in photosynthesis.","Light energy splits water molecules|splits water|water is split|splitting water","Releasing oxygen and supplying electrons and protons for the light-dependent reactions|oxygen|electrons|protons|hydrogen ions|replace electrons",{model:"In photolysis, light energy splits water, releasing oxygen and providing electrons and protons for the light-dependent reactions.",tip:"The oxygen released comes from water."}],
["C1.3","Outline",2,"Outline how carbon dioxide is fixed in the Calvin cycle.","Rubisco combines CO₂ with RuBP|rubisco|rubp|ribulose","Forming glycerate-3-phosphate, which is reduced to triose phosphate using ATP and NADPH|glycerate|gp|triose phosphate|nadph|reduced",{model:"Rubisco combines CO₂ with RuBP to form glycerate-3-phosphate, which is reduced to triose phosphate using ATP and NADPH.",tip:"Name the enzyme and the products."}],
["C1.3","Explain",2,"Explain how chromatography separates photosynthetic pigments.","Pigments differ in solubility and attraction to the paper|solubility|soluble|attraction|absorbed","So they travel different distances (different Rf values), showing a leaf contains several pigments|different distances|rf|several pigments|bands|spots",{model:"Pigments differ in their solubility in the solvent and their attraction to the paper, so they travel different distances, showing that leaves contain several pigments.",tip:"Rf = distance moved by pigment ÷ distance moved by solvent."}],
["C1.3","Outline",2,"Outline what an action spectrum shows.","The rate of photosynthesis at each wavelength of light|rate of photosynthesis|wavelength|each wavelength","It is highest in blue and red light and lowest in green|blue|red|green",{model:"An action spectrum shows the rate of photosynthesis at each wavelength of light: highest in blue and red, lowest in green.",tip:"Compare it with the absorption spectrum."}],
["C2.1","Outline",2,"Outline how adrenaline prepares the body for 'fight or flight'.","It increases heart rate and breathing rate|heart rate|breathing|ventilation","It raises blood glucose and diverts blood to the muscles|glucose|blood to muscles|muscles|glycogen breakdown",{model:"Adrenaline increases heart rate and breathing rate, raises blood glucose, and diverts blood to the muscles.",tip:"Give two effects."}],
["C2.1","Outline",2,"Outline the role of acetylcholine.","A neurotransmitter released at synapses, including neuromuscular junctions|neurotransmitter|synapse|synaptic|neuromuscular","It binds receptors on the next cell, causing depolarization, then is broken down by an enzyme|binds to receptors|depolarization|depolarisation|acetylcholinesterase|cholinesterase",{model:"Acetylcholine is a neurotransmitter released at synapses; it binds receptors on the next cell, causing depolarization, and is then broken down by acetylcholinesterase.",tip:"Release, bind, break down."}],
["C2.1","Distinguish",2,"Distinguish between endocrine and paracrine signalling.","Endocrine signals (hormones) travel in the blood to distant target cells|blood|distant|bloodstream|far","Paracrine signals act on nearby cells by diffusing a short distance|nearby|short distance|neighbouring|local|adjacent",{model:"Endocrine hormones travel in the blood to distant target cells; paracrine signals diffuse a short distance to act on nearby cells.",tip:"Distance is the difference."}],
["C2.1","Outline",2,"Outline how the insulin receptor brings about a response.","Insulin binds to a receptor on the cell surface (a tyrosine kinase)|binds|cell surface|tyrosine kinase|transmembrane","This triggers a signalling cascade that moves glucose transporters to the membrane|glucose transporters|glut4|glucose uptake|cascade|transporters",{model:"Insulin binds to a tyrosine kinase receptor on the cell surface, triggering a cascade that moves glucose transporters into the membrane, increasing glucose uptake.",tip:"Peptide hormones act at the surface."}],
["C2.2","Outline",2,"Outline repolarization during an action potential.","Potassium channels open and K⁺ diffuses out of the axon|potassium|k+|diffuses out","So the inside of the axon becomes negative again|negative|returns|restores",{model:"In repolarization, potassium channels open and K⁺ diffuses out of the axon, so the inside becomes negative again.",tip:"Na⁺ in = depolarization; K⁺ out = repolarization."}],
["C2.2","Outline",2,"Outline how neonicotinoid pesticides affect synaptic transmission in insects.","They bind irreversibly to acetylcholine receptors|bind|receptors|acetylcholine receptors|irreversibly","So the receptors stay activated or blocked, causing paralysis and death|paralysis|death|overstimulation|blocked|can t be broken down",{model:"Neonicotinoids bind irreversibly to acetylcholine receptors in insect synapses and are not broken down, causing paralysis and death.",tip:"Concern: their effect on bees."}],
["C2.2","Outline",2,"Outline how cocaine affects synaptic transmission.","It blocks the reuptake transporters for dopamine|blocks reuptake|reuptake|transporters|dopamine","So dopamine stays in the synaptic cleft, causing continued stimulation|stays|remains|continued|prolonged|overstimulation",{model:"Cocaine blocks the transporters that reabsorb dopamine, so dopamine stays in the synaptic cleft and keeps stimulating the next neuron.",tip:"Name the neurotransmitter."}],
["C2.2","Define",2,"Define threshold potential.","The membrane potential (about −50 mV) that must be reached|50 mv|must be reached|minimum","For an action potential to be triggered (all or nothing)|action potential|all or nothing|triggered|depolarization",{model:"The threshold potential (about −50 mV) is the membrane potential that must be reached for an action potential to be triggered; it is all or nothing.",tip:"Include a value."}],
["C3.1","Outline",2,"Outline the role of the cerebellum.","It coordinates skeletal muscle movement|coordinates|coordination|movement","And maintains balance and posture|balance|posture",{model:"The cerebellum coordinates skeletal muscle movement and maintains balance and posture.",tip:"Short answers are fine for an Outline."}],
["C3.1","Outline",3,"Outline the pain withdrawal reflex arc.","A pain receptor detects the stimulus and a sensory neuron carries the impulse to the spinal cord|sensory neuron|spinal cord|receptor","A relay neuron in the grey matter passes it to a motor neuron|relay|interneuron|grey matter","The motor neuron stimulates an effector muscle to contract, withdrawing the hand|motor neuron|effector|muscle contracts|withdraw",{model:"A pain receptor detects the stimulus; a sensory neuron carries the impulse to the spinal cord, where a relay neuron passes it to a motor neuron, which makes a muscle contract to withdraw the hand.",tip:"Receptor → sensory → relay → motor → effector."}],
["C3.1","Outline",2,"Outline how melatonin controls sleep patterns.","It is secreted by the pineal gland in darkness|pineal|dark|darkness|night","It sets the circadian rhythm, making us feel ready to sleep|circadian|rhythm|body clock|drowsy|tired",{model:"The pineal gland secretes melatonin in darkness, setting the circadian rhythm and making us feel ready to sleep.",tip:"Light suppresses melatonin."}],
["C3.1","Outline",2,"Outline how ventilation rate increases during exercise.","Chemoreceptors detect a rise in blood CO₂ (a fall in pH)|chemoreceptors|co2|carbon dioxide|ph","The medulla sends impulses to the diaphragm and intercostal muscles to contract faster and harder|medulla|brainstem|diaphragm|intercostal|impulses",{model:"Chemoreceptors detect the rise in blood CO₂ (fall in pH), and the medulla sends more impulses to the diaphragm and intercostal muscles, so breathing becomes faster and deeper.",tip:"Receptor → control centre → effector."}],
["C3.2","Outline",2,"Outline the role of B lymphocytes.","Each recognizes one specific antigen|specific antigen|antigen|specific","When activated they divide into plasma cells, which secrete antibodies, and memory cells|plasma cells|antibodies|memory cells|clone|clonal",{model:"Each B lymphocyte recognizes one specific antigen. When activated it divides into plasma cells that secrete antibodies and memory cells for future protection.",tip:"Clonal selection."}],
["C3.2","Outline",2,"Outline the role of helper T cells.","They recognize antigens presented by macrophages|antigen presenting|macrophages|presented","And activate the specific B cells, which then divide|activate|b cells|b lymphocytes|cytokines",{model:"Helper T cells recognize antigens presented by macrophages and activate the matching B cells, which then divide.",tip:"HIV destroys helper T cells."}],
["C3.2","Explain",2,"Explain why antibiotics should not be overused.","Overuse selects for resistant bacteria|resistant|resistance|selects","Making infections harder to treat, e.g. MRSA|harder to treat|mrsa|untreatable|superbugs",{model:"Overusing antibiotics selects for resistant bacteria, which survive and multiply, making infections such as MRSA harder to treat.",tip:"Natural selection in action."}],
["C3.2","Outline",2,"Outline herd immunity.","When a high proportion of a population has been vaccinated or infected|high proportion|most of|vaccinated|percentage","The pathogen can't spread easily, so unvaccinated people are also protected|can t spread|unvaccinated|protected|chains of transmission|indirectly",{model:"When most of a population is vaccinated, the pathogen can't spread easily, so people who can't be vaccinated are also protected.",tip:"It protects the vulnerable indirectly."}],
["C4.1","Define",2,"Define population.","A group of organisms of the same species|same species|one species","Living in the same area at the same time and able to interbreed|same area|same time|interbreed|place",{model:"A population is a group of organisms of the same species living in the same area at the same time.",tip:"Same species, same place, same time."}],
["C4.1","Outline",2,"Outline how predator and prey populations affect each other.","When prey numbers rise, predators have more food and increase|more food|predators increase|rise","More predators then reduce prey numbers, so predators later decline: a repeating cycle|decline|fall|cycle|lag|oscillate",{model:"When prey increase, predators have more food and increase too; more predators then reduce the prey, so predators later decline, giving repeating cycles.",tip:"Predator peaks lag behind prey peaks."}],
["C4.1","Outline",2,"Outline what is meant by carrying capacity.","The maximum population size an environment can support|maximum|largest|support","Limited by resources such as food, water and space|resources|food|space|limiting factors",{model:"Carrying capacity is the maximum population the environment can support, set by limited resources such as food and space.",tip:"It is the plateau of the sigmoid curve."}],
["C4.1","Distinguish",2,"Distinguish between density-dependent and density-independent factors.","Density-dependent factors have more effect as density rises, e.g. competition or disease|competition|disease|predation|more effect|increases with","Density-independent factors act regardless of density, e.g. floods, fire or temperature|regardless|floods|fire|weather|temperature|natural disaster",{model:"Density-dependent factors such as competition and disease have more effect as population density rises; density-independent factors such as floods affect a population whatever its density.",tip:"Give an example of each."}],
["C4.2","Outline",2,"Outline how carbon came to be stored in fossil fuels.","Dead organic matter didn't decompose fully in waterlogged or anaerobic conditions|incomplete decomposition|not decomposed|waterlogged|anaerobic|acidic","Over millions of years it was compressed into coal, oil and gas|millions of years|compressed|coal|oil|peat",{model:"Dead organic matter in waterlogged, anaerobic conditions didn't fully decompose and, over millions of years, was compressed into coal, oil and gas.",tip:"Burning releases this ancient carbon."}],
["C4.2","Outline",2,"Outline what a pyramid of energy shows.","The energy at each trophic level per unit area per year|each trophic level|per year|per unit area","Each level is smaller, as about 90% of the energy is lost between levels|smaller|90|lost|decreases",{model:"A pyramid of energy shows the energy at each trophic level per unit area per year; each level is smaller because about 90% is lost between levels.",tip:"It can never be inverted."}],
["C4.2","Define",2,"Define primary production.","The energy or biomass stored by plants and other autotrophs|plants|autotrophs|stored","Through photosynthesis, per unit area per unit time|photosynthesis|per unit area|per year|rate",{model:"Primary production is the energy (or biomass) stored by autotrophs through photosynthesis, per unit area per unit time.",tip:"Gross versus net: net subtracts respiration."}],
["C4.2","Outline",2,"Outline how methane is produced and removed in the carbon cycle.","Methanogenic archaea produce it in anaerobic conditions, e.g. wetlands and cattle guts|methanogen|methanogenic|archaea|anaerobic|wetlands|cattle","It is oxidized to CO₂ and water in the atmosphere|oxidized|oxidised|carbon dioxide|co2|breaks down",{model:"Methanogenic archaea produce methane in anaerobic places such as wetlands and cattle guts; in the atmosphere it is gradually oxidized to CO₂ and water.",tip:"Methane is a strong greenhouse gas."}],
["D1.1","Outline",3,"Outline the polymerase chain reaction (PCR).","DNA is heated to about 95 °C to separate the strands|95|heated|separate|denature","It is cooled to about 55 °C so primers bind|55|primers|anneal|cooled","Taq polymerase extends the new strands at about 72 °C, and the cycle repeats|72|taq|extends|repeat",{model:"DNA is heated to about 95 °C to separate the strands, cooled to about 55 °C so primers bind, then Taq polymerase builds new strands at about 72 °C; repeating the cycle doubles the DNA each time.",tip:"Three temperatures, three steps."}],
["D1.1","Outline",2,"Outline gel electrophoresis of DNA.","DNA fragments are placed in wells and an electric current is applied|wells|electric|current","Negatively charged DNA moves towards the positive electrode, and smaller fragments move further|smaller|further|faster|size|positive electrode",{model:"DNA fragments are loaded into wells and a current is applied; the negatively charged DNA moves towards the positive electrode, with smaller fragments moving further.",tip:"Separation by size."}],
["D1.1","Outline",2,"Outline one use of DNA profiling.","A named use, e.g. forensics (crime scenes) or paternity testing|crime|forensic|suspect|paternity","Band patterns of the DNA samples are compared to see whether they match|bands|band pattern|banding|same bands",{model:"In forensics, DNA from a crime scene is compared with suspects' DNA to see whether the band patterns match.",tip:"Name the use and what is compared."}],
["D1.1","Outline",2,"Outline the role of primase in DNA replication.","It makes a short RNA primer complementary to the template|rna primer|primer|short rna","Giving DNA polymerase a starting point to add nucleotides|starting point|start|dna polymerase",{model:"Primase makes a short RNA primer complementary to the template, giving DNA polymerase a starting point.",tip:"DNA polymerase can't start a new strand on its own."}],
["D1.2","Outline",2,"Outline the role of RNA polymerase.","It unwinds the DNA and separates the strands at the gene|unwinds|separates|unzips","And joins RNA nucleotides complementary to the template strand, 5′ to 3′|nucleotides|complementary|template|5 to 3|mrna",{model:"RNA polymerase unwinds and separates the DNA strands at a gene and joins RNA nucleotides complementary to the template strand, 5′ to 3′.",tip:"No primer is needed."}],
["D1.2","Outline",2,"Outline how mRNA is processed in eukaryotes before translation.","Introns are removed by splicing|introns|splicing|spliced","A 5′ cap and a poly-A tail are added|cap|poly a|tail",{model:"Introns are removed by splicing, and a 5′ cap and a poly-A tail are added.",tip:"Exons are kept and expressed."}],
["D1.2","Outline",2,"Outline the role of ribosomes in translation.","They bind mRNA and move along it codon by codon|move along|codon|binds mrna","They catalyse peptide bonds between amino acids brought by tRNA|peptide bonds|amino acids|trna",{model:"Ribosomes bind mRNA and move along it codon by codon, catalysing peptide bonds between the amino acids brought by tRNA.",tip:"Ribosomes are made of rRNA and protein."}],
["D1.2","Explain",2,"Explain why a single base substitution may not change the protein produced.","The genetic code is degenerate: several codons code for the same amino acid|degenerate|same amino acid|more than one codon","So the new codon may still code for the same amino acid (a silent mutation)|silent|no change|same protein|third base",{model:"Because the genetic code is degenerate, the new codon may code for the same amino acid, so the protein is unchanged: a silent mutation.",tip:"Often the third base of the codon."}],
["D1.3","Outline",2,"Outline how ultraviolet radiation causes mutations.","UV makes adjacent thymine bases bond, forming dimers|thymine dimers|dimers|thymine","Which distort the DNA and cause copying errors, e.g. leading to skin cancer|errors|copying|replication|skin cancer|distort",{model:"UV radiation causes adjacent thymine bases to bond into dimers, which distort the DNA and cause errors when it is copied, leading to skin cancer.",tip:"UV is a mutagen."}],
["D1.3","Outline",2,"Outline one ethical issue with gene editing in humans.","Editing germ-line cells affects future generations, who can't consent|germ line|future generations|consent|inherited","And it could lead to 'designer babies' or have unknown long-term effects|designer babies|enhancement|unknown effects|off target|long term",{model:"Editing germ-line cells changes the DNA of future generations, who can't consent, and the long-term effects are unknown.",tip:"Somatic editing affects only the patient."}],
["D1.3","Explain",2,"Explain why most mutations have no effect or are damaging.","Many are in non-coding DNA or are silent|non coding|silent|no effect|introns","Changes to coding sequences usually disrupt a protein that already works well|disrupt|non functional|working protein|changes the protein|faulty",{model:"Many mutations occur in non-coding DNA or are silent, so they have no effect; mutations in coding DNA usually disrupt a protein that already works well.",tip:"Beneficial mutations are rare."}],
["D1.3","Outline",2,"Outline the role of the guide RNA in CRISPR–Cas9.","It is complementary to the target DNA sequence|complementary|target|matches","It directs Cas9 to cut the DNA at that exact place|cas9|cut|directs|leads",{model:"The guide RNA is complementary to the target DNA sequence and leads Cas9 to cut the DNA at exactly that place.",tip:"The guide RNA gives specificity."}],
["D2.1","Outline",2,"Outline what happens during interphase.","The cell grows and carries out its normal functions (G1 and G2)|grows|growth|g1|g2","DNA is replicated in the S phase|s phase|replicated|replication|copied",{model:"In interphase the cell grows and carries out its normal functions (G1, G2), and replicates its DNA in the S phase.",tip:"Interphase is not resting."}],
["D2.1","Outline",2,"Outline the role of the spindle in mitosis.","Microtubule fibres attach to the centromeres of the chromosomes|microtubules|centromeres|attach|kinetochore","And pull sister chromatids to opposite poles|pull|opposite poles|separate|chromatids",{model:"Spindle microtubules attach to chromosome centromeres and pull the sister chromatids to opposite poles.",tip:"This happens in anaphase."}],
["D2.1","Explain",2,"Explain how meiosis halves the chromosome number.","In the first division, homologous chromosomes are separated|homologous|pairs separated|first division","So each cell receives one chromosome from each pair (haploid)|one from each pair|haploid|half",{model:"In meiosis I homologous chromosomes are separated, so each new cell gets one chromosome from each pair and becomes haploid.",tip:"Meiosis I is the reduction division."}],
["D2.1","Outline",2,"Outline how cancer can result from uncontrolled cell division.","Mutations in genes that control the cell cycle (oncogenes)|oncogenes|mutations|cell cycle|tumour suppressor","Cells divide repeatedly, forming a tumour that may spread (metastasis)|tumour|tumor|metastasis|spread|repeatedly",{model:"Mutations in genes that control the cell cycle, such as oncogenes, make cells divide repeatedly, forming a tumour that may spread to other tissues.",tip:"Mutation → uncontrolled division → tumour."}],
["D2.2","Outline",2,"Outline how environmental factors can affect gene expression, with an example.","Factors such as temperature, diet or chemicals switch genes on or off|temperature|diet|chemicals|environment|switch","E.g. Himalayan rabbits grow black fur only on their cold extremities|himalayan|rabbit|fur|hydrangea|cold",{model:"Environmental factors can switch genes on or off; in Himalayan rabbits, a temperature-sensitive enzyme makes black fur only on the cold ears, nose and feet.",tip:"Give a named example."}],
["D2.2","Outline",2,"Outline the role of promoters in gene expression.","A DNA sequence before a gene where RNA polymerase binds|rna polymerase|binds|upstream|before the gene","Starting transcription; how strongly it binds affects how much the gene is expressed|transcription|starts|initiates|rate",{model:"A promoter is a DNA sequence just before a gene where RNA polymerase binds to start transcription; its strength affects how much the gene is expressed.",tip:"Promoters are not transcribed."}],
["D2.2","Outline",2,"Outline genomic imprinting.","Some genes are expressed only from the copy inherited from one parent|one parent|mother|father|parental","Because the other copy is silenced by methylation|methylation|silenced|switched off",{model:"In genomic imprinting a gene is expressed only from the copy inherited from one parent, because the other copy is silenced by methylation.",tip:"An example of epigenetics."}],
["D2.2","Outline",2,"Outline how histone acetylation affects gene expression.","Acetyl groups added to histones loosen the chromatin|loosen|loosens|open|less tightly","So transcription factors and RNA polymerase can reach the DNA, increasing transcription|increases|transcription|access|expressed",{model:"Adding acetyl groups to histones loosens the chromatin, so transcription factors and RNA polymerase can reach the DNA and transcription increases.",tip:"Acetylation → on; methylation → usually off."}],
["D2.3","Define",2,"Define solute potential.","The part of water potential due to dissolved substances|dissolved|due to","It is always negative, and more solute makes it more negative|negative|more negative|always negative",{model:"Solute potential is the component of water potential due to dissolved solutes; it is always negative and becomes more negative as concentration increases.",tip:"Pure water has a solute potential of zero."}],
["D2.3","Explain",2,"Explain why animal cells burst in pure water but plant cells don't.","Water enters both types of cell by osmosis|osmosis|water enters","The plant cell wall resists expansion (turgor pressure), but animal cells have no wall|cell wall|wall|turgor|pressure potential",{model:"Water enters both cells by osmosis, but the plant cell wall resists expansion, building turgor pressure; animal cells have no wall, so they burst.",tip:"The wall is the difference."}],
["D2.3","Outline",2,"Outline how to find the water potential of a plant tissue experimentally.","Place samples in a range of sucrose or salt solutions and measure the percentage change in mass|range of concentrations|sucrose|salt|change in mass|percentage change","The concentration with no change in mass has the same water potential as the tissue|no change|zero change|isotonic|intercept|same water potential",{model:"Place tissue samples in a range of sucrose solutions, measure the percentage change in mass, and find where the graph crosses zero: that solution has the same water potential as the tissue.",tip:"Use percentage change, not mass change."}],
["D2.3","Outline",2,"Outline why isotonic solutions are used in medicine.","They have the same water potential as body fluids|same concentration|same solute|same water potential","So cells neither gain nor lose water, e.g. in IV drips and organ storage|neither|no net|drips|transplant|organs|don t burst",{model:"Isotonic solutions have the same water potential as body fluids, so cells neither gain nor lose water; they are used in IV drips and to store organs for transplant.",tip:"Give a medical use."}],
["D3.1","Outline",2,"Outline the role of the placenta.","Exchanges oxygen, nutrients and wastes between mother and fetus|exchange|oxygen|nutrients|wastes","Without their blood mixing; it also secretes hormones such as progesterone|blood doesn t mix|not mix|progesterone|hormones|oestrogen",{model:"The placenta exchanges oxygen, nutrients and wastes between mother and fetus without their blood mixing, and secretes progesterone.",tip:"Exchange plus hormones."}],
["D3.1","Outline",3,"Outline in vitro fertilization (IVF).","Hormones stimulate the ovaries to release many eggs|hormones|stimulate|many eggs|superovulation","Eggs are collected and mixed with sperm in a dish|collected|dish|outside the body|sperm","Embryos are placed in the uterus|uterus|implanted|transferred",{model:"Hormones stimulate the ovaries to produce many eggs, which are collected and fertilized with sperm in a dish; resulting embryos are placed in the uterus.",tip:"In vitro = outside the body."}],
["D3.1","Outline",2,"Outline the role of progesterone in the menstrual cycle.","Secreted by the corpus luteum after ovulation|corpus luteum|after ovulation","It maintains the uterus lining and inhibits FSH and LH|maintains|lining|endometrium|inhibits|fsh|lh",{model:"Progesterone is secreted by the corpus luteum after ovulation; it maintains the uterus lining and inhibits FSH and LH.",tip:"Falling progesterone triggers menstruation."}],
["D3.1","Distinguish",2,"Distinguish between pollination and fertilization in plants.","Pollination is the transfer of pollen from an anther to a stigma|anther|stigma|transfer","Fertilization is the fusion of male and female gametes in the ovule|fusion|gametes|ovule|nuclei",{model:"Pollination is the transfer of pollen from anther to stigma; fertilization is the later fusion of male and female gametes inside the ovule.",tip:"Pollination comes first."}],
["D3.2","Outline",2,"Outline what a pedigree chart shows.","The inheritance of a trait through generations of a family|generations|family|inheritance","Using squares for males, circles for females and shading for affected individuals|squares|circles|shaded|affected",{model:"A pedigree chart shows how a trait is inherited through generations of a family, with squares for males, circles for females and shading for affected people.",tip:"Learn the symbols."}],
["D3.2","Explain",2,"Explain why a recessive allele can stay hidden for generations.","Heterozygous carriers don't show the trait|carriers|heterozygous|carrier","It only appears when two carriers have a child who inherits two copies|homozygous|two carriers|both parents|two copies",{model:"Heterozygous carriers don't show a recessive trait, so it only appears when two carriers have a child who inherits two copies.",tip:"Cystic fibrosis is an example."}],
["D3.2","Outline",2,"Outline polygenic inheritance.","A trait controlled by several genes|several genes|many genes|multiple genes","Giving continuous variation, e.g. height or skin colour|continuous|height|skin colour|range|normal distribution",{model:"In polygenic inheritance several genes control one trait, giving continuous variation such as human height.",tip:"Continuous variation gives a bell curve."}],
["D3.2","Outline",2,"Outline the ABO blood group system as an example of multiple alleles.","There are three alleles: Iᴬ, Iᴮ and i|three alleles|3 alleles|ia ib","Iᴬ and Iᴮ are codominant, and both are dominant to i|codominant|codominance|dominant to i|recessive",{model:"There are three alleles, Iᴬ, Iᴮ and i; Iᴬ and Iᴮ are codominant and both are dominant to i.",tip:"Blood group AB shows codominance."}],
["D3.3","Outline",2,"Outline how blood glucose is raised when it falls too low.","Alpha cells of the pancreas secrete glucagon|glucagon|alpha cells","Which makes the liver break down glycogen into glucose|liver|glycogen|breaks down|glycogenolysis",{model:"Alpha cells in the pancreas secrete glucagon, which makes the liver break down glycogen into glucose and release it.",tip:"Glucagon raises; insulin lowers."}],
["D3.3","Outline",2,"Outline the role of ADH.","Released by the pituitary when the blood is too concentrated|pituitary|concentrated|dehydrated|low water","It makes the collecting ducts more permeable, so more water is reabsorbed|collecting ducts|permeable|reabsorbed|aquaporins|less urine",{model:"When the blood is too concentrated, the pituitary releases ADH, which makes the collecting ducts more permeable so more water is reabsorbed.",tip:"ADH → less, more concentrated urine."}],
["D3.3","Distinguish",2,"Distinguish between type 1 and type 2 diabetes.","Type 1: the immune system destroys beta cells, so no insulin is made|no insulin|immune|autoimmune|beta cells","Type 2: cells stop responding to insulin, often linked to obesity|stop responding|resistance|insensitive|obesity|lifestyle",{model:"In type 1 diabetes the immune system destroys beta cells, so no insulin is made. In type 2, cells stop responding to insulin, often linked to obesity.",tip:"Production versus response."}],
["D3.3","Outline",2,"Outline the role of the hypothalamus in controlling body temperature.","It monitors the temperature of the blood|monitors|detects|thermoreceptors","And triggers responses such as sweating or shivering|sweating|shivering|vasodilation|vasoconstriction",{model:"The hypothalamus monitors blood temperature and triggers responses such as sweating and vasodilation, or shivering and vasoconstriction.",tip:"It is the control centre."}],
["D4.1","Outline",2,"Outline sexual selection.","Traits that improve mating success are selected|mating success|mates|attract","Even if they reduce survival, e.g. the peacock's tail|peacock|tail|reduce survival|display",{model:"In sexual selection, traits that increase mating success are selected even if they reduce survival, like the peacock's tail.",tip:"A peacock or bird-of-paradise example helps."}],
["D4.1","Distinguish",2,"Distinguish between natural and artificial selection.","In natural selection the environment determines which individuals survive and reproduce|environment|survive|nature","In artificial selection humans choose which individuals breed|humans|breeders|choose|selective breeding",{model:"In natural selection the environment determines which individuals survive to reproduce; in artificial selection humans choose which individuals breed.",tip:"Who does the choosing?"}],
["D4.1","Outline",2,"Outline genetic drift.","Random changes in allele frequencies|random|chance|allele frequencies","Strongest in small populations, e.g. after a bottleneck|small populations|bottleneck|founder effect",{model:"Genetic drift is random change in allele frequencies, strongest in small populations such as after a bottleneck.",tip:"It isn't caused by selection."}],
["D4.1","Outline",2,"Outline how a population's gene pool changes during evolution.","A gene pool is all the alleles in a population|all the alleles|all alleles|population","Selection, drift and mutation change allele frequencies over time|allele frequencies|frequencies|selection|drift|mutation",{model:"The gene pool is all the alleles in a population; natural selection, genetic drift and mutation change their frequencies over time.",tip:"Evolution = change in allele frequency."}],
["D4.2","Outline",2,"Outline secondary succession.","Succession where soil remains after a disturbance such as fire or farming|soil remains|existing soil|fire|abandoned|disturbance","So it is faster than primary succession|faster|quicker|no pioneer stage",{model:"Secondary succession happens where soil remains after a disturbance such as fire or abandoned farming, so it is faster than primary succession.",tip:"Soil is already present."}],
["D4.2","Outline",2,"Outline what is meant by an ecological tipping point.","A threshold beyond which an ecosystem changes to a new state|threshold|new state|beyond","Which it can't easily return from, e.g. rainforest turning to savanna|irreversible|can t return|savanna|coral reef|collapse",{model:"A tipping point is a threshold beyond which an ecosystem shifts to a new state it can't easily return from, such as the Amazon turning into savanna.",tip:"Give an example."}],
["D4.2","Outline",2,"Outline how deforestation affects ecosystem stability.","It removes habitats and reduces biodiversity|habitat|biodiversity|species","It increases soil erosion and disrupts the water and carbon cycles|erosion|water cycle|carbon|rainfall|nutrients",{model:"Deforestation removes habitats, reducing biodiversity, and increases soil erosion while disrupting the water and carbon cycles.",tip:"Biological and physical effects."}],
["D4.2","Outline",2,"Outline one way agriculture can be made more sustainable.","E.g. crop rotation, reduced fertiliser use or integrated pest management|crop rotation|organic|integrated pest|less fertiliser|less fertilizer|cover crops|no till","Protecting soil, water and biodiversity for the future|soil fertility|soil|biodiversity|long term|future generations",{model:"Crop rotation and reduced fertiliser use maintain soil fertility and protect water and biodiversity for the future.",tip:"Method plus benefit."}],
["D4.3","Outline",2,"Outline how ocean acidification affects marine organisms.","CO₂ dissolves to form carbonic acid, lowering pH|carbonic acid|lower ph|ph falls|co2 dissolves","This reduces carbonate ions, so corals and shellfish struggle to build calcium carbonate|calcium carbonate|shells|corals|carbonate",{model:"CO₂ dissolves in seawater forming carbonic acid, lowering pH and reducing carbonate ions, so corals and shellfish struggle to build calcium carbonate skeletons and shells.",tip:"Chemistry, then biology."}],
["D4.3","Outline",2,"Outline how climate change can disrupt the timing of events between species.","Warming makes spring events such as budburst or insect emergence happen earlier|earlier|spring|budburst|emergence","But other species, such as nesting birds, may not shift, so food isn't available when needed|not available|birds|chicks|food|out of step",{model:"Warming makes events like insect emergence happen earlier, but birds may still breed at the same time, so food isn't available when their chicks need it.",tip:"This is called phenological mismatch."}],
["D4.3","Outline",2,"Outline the effect of rising temperatures on species distribution.","Species move towards the poles or to higher altitudes|poles|higher altitude|uphill|north","Species that can't move or adapt may decline or become extinct|extinct|extinction|decline",{model:"As temperatures rise, species shift towards the poles and to higher altitudes; those that can't move or adapt decline or become extinct.",tip:"Mountain species have nowhere further to go."}],
["D4.3","Outline",2,"Outline how thawing permafrost contributes to climate change.","Thawing lets microbes decompose the stored organic matter|decomposition|decomposers|organic matter|microbes","Releasing CO₂ and methane, which cause further warming (positive feedback)|methane|co2|carbon dioxide|positive feedback|further warming",{model:"As permafrost thaws, microbes decompose the stored organic matter, releasing CO₂ and methane, which cause further warming: a positive feedback.",tip:"Name the feedback."}],
]);
